(window.SITE_PARTS=window.SITE_PARTS||{})["bill-simplification"]={"plain":{"bills":{"simplification":{"slug":"simplification","title":"The Government Simplification Act","sections":[{"anchor":"sec-0-1","heading":"\u00a7 0-1. Short title.","part":"SECTION 0 \u2014 SHORT TITLE; FINDINGS; DEFINITIONS","body":"This Act may be cited as the *\"Government Simplification Act\"*.","partHeads":[{"heading":"SECTION 0 \u2014 SHORT TITLE; FINDINGS; DEFINITIONS","body":""}]},{"anchor":"sec-0-2","heading":"\u00a7 0-2. Findings.","part":"SECTION 0 \u2014 SHORT TITLE; FINDINGS; DEFINITIONS","body":"Congress finds the following:\n\n**(1)** In section 3506(c)(3) of title 44, United States Code, enacted in 1995, Congress required every agency to certify, for each collection of information, that the collection is not unnecessarily duplicative of information otherwise reasonably accessible to the agency, that it reduces burden to the extent practicable and appropriate, and that it is written using plain, coherent, and unambiguous terminology and is understandable to those who are to respond.\n\n**(2)** In the Plain Writing Act of 2010 (5 U.S.C. 301 note), Congress required each agency to use plain writing in every covered document the agency issues or substantially revises, and in section 6 of that Act provided that there shall be no judicial review of compliance or noncompliance with any provision of that Act and that no provision of that Act shall be construed to create any right or benefit enforceable by any administrative or judicial action.\n\n**(3)** In section 4(c) of the 21st Century Integrated Digital Experience Act (44 U.S.C. 3501 note), Congress required the head of each executive agency, not later than 2 years after the enactment of that Act, to ensure that any paper based form related to serving the public is made available in a digital format. That requirement was unqualified and its deadline passed in December 2020.\n\n**(4)** In sections 321 through 324 of title 5, United States Code, enacted in 2025, Congress required the Director of the Office of Management and Budget to develop and oversee Government service delivery standards that solicit and consider voluntary feedback and that account for ease, efficiency, transparency, accessibility, fairness, burden, and duration, including wait and processing times, and required the head of each agency to designate a senior official to implement that subchapter.\n\n**(5)** Each requirement described in paragraphs (1) through (4) remains in force. None requires that any measurement of the burden borne by the public be published. None provides for verification of an agency's compliance by any person outside the agency. None establishes a deadline by which an agency must answer a proposal to reduce a burden, or a consequence for failing to answer. The deficiency this Act addresses is therefore not an absence of duties but an absence of measurement, publication, and consequence.\n\n**(6)** The inventory of currently approved information collections maintained under chapter 35 of title 44, United States Code, reported approximately 11,400,000,000 annual burden hours and approximately $203,000,000,000 in annual cost across approximately 10,700 approved collections. Those figures are estimates prepared by the agencies that impose the collections.\n\n**(7)** The Comptroller General has reported that estimates of the kind described in paragraph (6) contained errors of substantial magnitude, that agencies frequently failed to convert burden hours into cost, and that neither the agencies' own reviews nor the Office of Management and Budget detected the errors. A measurement prepared and audited by the party whose conduct is measured does not reliably describe that conduct.\n\n**(8)** Under section 3502(3)(A)(i) of title 44, United States Code, a question posed to ten or more persons is a collection of information requiring clearance, and the clearance sequence prescribed by sections 3506(c)(2) and 3507 of that title requires a 60-day agency notice, a comment period of not less than 30 days, and a decision period of 60 days. An agency seeking promptly to learn from the public which of its procedures are failing is therefore delayed by the chapter enacted to reduce paperwork.\n\n**(9)** The costs of administrative burden fall on persons who are numerous, unorganized, and individually affected in amounts too small to justify organized advocacy, while the effort required to reduce a burden falls on the agency that imposes it. Absent measurement and publication, the incentives of the agency and the interests of the public diverge, and the accumulated burden grows without any deliberate decision to increase it.\n\n**(10)** Administrative burden operates as a reduction in a benefit or protection without a vote of Congress. Requirements that are onerous to satisfy withhold from eligible persons what Congress has provided for them, and do so without amending the statute that provides it.\n\n**(11)** A person denied information that a statute entitles that person to receive suffers a concrete injury. That injury is of the same kind as the injury suffered by a person denied inspection of a public record, or denied an accounting, for which the courts of the United States and the courts from which they inherit their practice have long afforded relief. Congress enacts section 1-1(d) on that understanding."},{"anchor":"sec-0-3","heading":"\u00a7 0-3. Definitions.","part":"SECTION 0 \u2014 SHORT TITLE; FINDINGS; DEFINITIONS","body":"In this Act:\n\n**(a) Agency.**\u2014The term *\"agency\"* has the meaning given that term in section 3502(1) of title 44, United States Code. That definition includes an independent regulatory agency and excludes the Government Accountability Office, the Federal Election Commission, the governments of the District of Columbia and of the territories and possessions of the United States and their subdivisions, and Government-owned contractor-operated facilities. Congress adopts those exclusions for purposes of this Act as a determination that the entities excluded are not subject to this Act, and not as a gap.\n\n**(b) Covered process.**\u2014The term *\"covered process\"* means a discrete sequence of steps by which a person applies for, obtains, maintains, renews, reports under, appeals, or is determined eligible for a benefit, service, status, license, permit, registration, or authorization from an agency, where the agency records or reasonably estimates a number of interactions in a fiscal year not less than the threshold set in the schedule. The term includes such a sequence conducted in whole or in part by a contractor or nonprofit organization acting on behalf of the agency or administering a federally funded program.\n\n**(c) Interaction.**\u2014The term *\"interaction\"* means a person's engagement with a covered process through a Government service delivery channel, as that term is defined in section 321(4) of title 5, United States Code, from the person's first submission of information or request through the agency's final action on it.\n\n**(c-1) Final action.**\u2014The term *\"final action\"* means the agency's grant, denial, or other disposition of the person's submission, or the agency's communication to the person that no further action will be taken on it. The term is defined by this subsection and not by section 704 of title 5, United States Code, or by any construction given to that section, and a submission may be the subject of a final action for purposes of this Act whether or not the action is final agency action for purposes of judicial review under any other provision of law.\n\n**(d) Interaction burden.**\u2014The term *\"interaction burden\"* means the time, effort, and financial resources a person expends in dealing with an agency, and consists of the following and only the following:\n\n**(1)** burden as defined in section 3502(2) of title 44, United States Code;\n\n**(2)** time elapsed between a person's submission and the agency's final action on it, including time during which the agency takes no action;\n\n**(3)** time expended appearing in person, waiting to appear, or waiting on a telephone or other channel to reach a person authorized to act;\n\n**(4)** resources expended obtaining, authenticating, translating, or correcting a document or record from a third party in order to satisfy the agency;\n\n**(5)** resources expended correcting an error made by the agency, or seeking reconsideration, appeal, or review of an agency action; and\n\n**(6)** resources expended by a person a second or subsequent time because a prior submission was lost, not acted upon, or required to be repeated.\n\n**(d-1) Relation to chapter 35 of title 44.**\u2014The term *\"interaction burden\"* is broader than the term *\"burden\"* as defined in section 3502(2) of title 44, United States Code. Nothing in this Act narrows that definition or the application of chapter 35 of title 44 to any collection of information. Where this Act uses the word *\"burden\"* without the modifier *\"interaction,\"* it means burden as defined in that section.\n\n**(e) Completion; abandonment; procedural denial.**\u2014\n\n**(1)** A covered process is *\"completed\"* when the agency takes final action on the person's submission.\n\n**(2)** A covered process is *\"abandoned\"* when a person who has begun it takes no further step within the period set in the schedule and the agency takes no final action on the merits.\n\n**(3)** A *\"procedural denial\"* is a final action denying, terminating, reducing, or declining to renew a benefit, service, status, license, permit, registration, or authorization on a ground other than the person's substantive eligibility or qualification, including failure to file within a period, failure to supply a document, failure to respond to a request for information, and failure to appear.\n\n**(f) The Office; the Advocate.**\u2014The terms *\"Office\"* and *\"Advocate\"* mean the Office of the Public Advocate for Government Simplification and the Public Advocate for Government Simplification.\n\n**(g) The Ledger.**\u2014The term *\"Ledger\"* means the Government Burden Ledger required by section 1-1. The Ledger is distinct from the schedule to this Act, and a reference in this Act to *\"the schedule\"* is a reference to that schedule and never to the Ledger.\n\n**(h) Unit of measurement.**\u2014The term *\"unit of measurement\"* means the definition, prescribed by the Advocate under section 1-4, of what constitutes one covered process, one interaction, one step, and one document for purposes of the Ledger.\n\n**(i) Tribal.**\u2014The term *\"Tribal government\"* means the governing body of any Indian Tribe as defined in section 5304 of title 25, United States Code. Where this Act enumerates Federal, State, and local governments in a provision that protects, recognizes, or empowers, the enumeration includes Tribal governments."},{"anchor":"sec-0-4","heading":"\u00a7 0-4. Rules of construction.","part":"SECTION 0 \u2014 SHORT TITLE; FINDINGS; DEFINITIONS","body":"**(a) Closed list.**\u2014The defenses, exceptions, and exclusions available under this Act are those and only those this Act provides. The common law supplies none. An omission from a list in this Act is a determination of Congress and is not a gap to be filled by construction.\n\n**(b) No authority to reduce.**\u2014Nothing in this Act authorizes an agency to reduce, condition, narrow, or terminate a benefit, service, status, right, or protection. Section 6-1 governs and its limits apply to every action taken under this Act.\n\n**(c) No duty on other governments.**\u2014Nothing in this Act imposes a duty on a State, local, or Tribal government. A provision of this Act extending a protection or a channel to an officer or employee of a State, local, or Tribal government confers that protection and imposes no obligation.\n\n**(d) Chief Information Officer authorities.**\u2014Nothing in this Act diminishes the authority of an agency Chief Information Officer under section 11315 of title 40, United States Code, or under section 3502 of title 44, United States Code."},{"anchor":"sec-1-1","heading":"\u00a7 1-1. Establishment; publication.","part":"SECTION 1 \u2014 THE GOVERNMENT BURDEN LEDGER","body":"**(a) Establishment.**\u2014There is established a public record, to be known as the Government Burden Ledger, which the Advocate shall maintain and publish.\n\n**(b) Contents.**\u2014Each agency shall furnish to the Advocate, and the Advocate shall publish in the Ledger, for each covered process of that agency\u2014\n\n**(1)** the number of interactions begun and the number completed in the fiscal year;\n\n**(2)** the median and the ninetieth-percentile time from a person's first submission to the agency's final action;\n\n**(3)** the median and the ninetieth-percentile time a person spends completing the process, measured under the unit of measurement;\n\n**(4)** the number of distinct steps required and the number of documents the person must supply;\n\n**(5)** the number and rate of abandonments;\n\n**(6)** the number and rate of procedural denials, stated separately from denials on the merits;\n\n**(7)** the number of submissions a person was required to make a second or subsequent time, and the reason;\n\n**(8)** the number of full-time equivalent employees and the number of contractor personnel assigned to the process, and the number of pending matters at the close of the fiscal year; and\n\n**(9)** an estimate, prepared under subsection (b-1), of the number of persons eligible for the benefit or service and the number enrolled or served; and\n\n**(10)** each form the covered process requires that is not available in a digital format meeting the requirements of section 3(a) of the 21st Century Integrated Digital Experience Act (44 U.S.C. 3501 note), and, for each, the documentation required by section 4(d) of that Act.\n\n**(b-1) Participation estimates; no linked record.**\u2014An estimate under subsection (b)(9) shall be prepared, in the following order of preference, from an estimate of program participation already published by an agency or by the Comptroller General, or from a statistical sample or a household survey. **No agency and no person acting on its behalf may create, obtain, or retain a record linking individually identifiable information across programs or agencies for the purpose of preparing an estimate under this Act.** Where no estimate can be prepared by a means this subsection permits, the agency shall state that fact and the reason, and subsection (e) is satisfied by that statement.\n\n**(c) Form of publication.**\u2014Publication under this section shall be in a machine-readable format, without charge, without registration, and without a request under section 552 of title 5, United States Code.\n\n**(c-1) Aggregate figures only; research access.**\u2014The Ledger shall contain aggregate figures and shall not contain information that identifies or can reasonably be used to identify an individual. The Advocate shall preserve the data underlying each published figure and shall make it available for research in a form from which individually identifiable information has been removed by expert determination. The Advocate shall obtain and publish the determination, including the qualifications of the person making it and the methods applied. Nothing in this section authorizes a disclosure that section 552a of title 5, United States Code, subchapter III of chapter 35 of title 44, United States Code, or section 6103 of the Internal Revenue Code of 1986 prohibits, and the omission from this Act of any exception to those provisions is a determination of Congress.\n\n**(d) Ministerial duty; entitlement; enforcement.**\u2014\n\n**(1) Entitlement.**\u2014Each person is entitled to the information this section requires to be published. That entitlement is created by this section and does not depend on the construction given to any other statute.\n\n**(2) Injury.**\u2014A person who is denied information to which paragraph (1) entitles that person is injured by the denial. The injury is the denial of the information itself, and no further showing of harm is required.\n\n**(3) Duty nondiscretionary.**\u2014Publication under this section is a ministerial duty. It is not committed to agency discretion within the meaning of section 701(a)(2) of title 5, United States Code. A provision of law enacted before the date of enactment of this Act that precludes or limits judicial review does not reach an action under this subsection, and the omission of any such provision from this paragraph is a determination of Congress and not a gap.\n\n**(4) Relief.**\u2014A person described in paragraph (2) may bring a civil action in an appropriate district court of the United States, and in an action in the nature of mandamus. On a finding that the information required by this section has not been published, the court **shall** order it published and **shall** set a date for compliance. Relief under this paragraph is not discretionary, and neither the burden of compliance nor the availability of the information from another source is a ground for withholding it.\n\n**(5) No exhaustion; no channeling.**\u2014A person need not request the information, seek reconsideration, or pursue any administrative remedy before bringing an action under this subsection. No administrative scheme, and no other provision of law, is the exclusive means of obtaining the information this section requires to be published.\n\n**(6) Mootness.**\u2014Publication after an action is brought does not moot the action as to costs and fees, and does not moot the action as to future fiscal years where the information has not been published in each of the two most recent fiscal years.\n\n**(7) Limitation.**\u2014An action under this subsection shall be brought within the period set in the schedule, running from the date on which the information was required to be published. Where the information has not been published, a new period begins on each date on which publication is again required.\n\n**(e) What does not satisfy this section.**\u2014An agency does not satisfy subsection (b) by furnishing a figure the agency does not compute under the unit of measurement, by furnishing a category in place of a figure, by aggregating covered processes the unit of measurement treats as distinct, or by omitting an element of subsection (b) without stating that the element is unavailable, why it is unavailable, and the date by which it will be available.\n\n**(e-1) Unavailable elements; transition.**\u2014An agency that states an element unavailable under subsection (e) shall furnish that element for each fiscal year beginning after the transition period set in the schedule. The Advocate shall publish annually each element reported unavailable, by agency and covered process, the reason given, the date furnished, and the number of consecutive years the element has been reported unavailable. Beginning at the end of the transition period, a statement of unavailability does not satisfy subsection (b), and the entry is incomplete for purposes of section 1-3.","partHeads":[{"heading":"SECTION 1 \u2014 THE GOVERNMENT BURDEN LEDGER","body":""}]},{"anchor":"sec-1-2","heading":"\u00a7 1-2. Verification.","part":"SECTION 1 \u2014 THE GOVERNMENT BURDEN LEDGER","body":"**(a) Verification required.**\u2014The Advocate shall verify the figures furnished under section 1-1(b). Verification shall be by examination of the agency's records and systems and shall not rely on the agency's certification.\n\n**(b) Frequency.**\u2014The Advocate shall verify each covered process not less frequently than the interval set in the schedule, and shall verify annually a sample of covered processes drawn so that every agency is represented.\n\n**(c) Correction.**\u2014Where the Advocate determines that a published figure is materially inaccurate, the Advocate shall publish the corrected figure, the figure the agency furnished, and the reason for the difference.\n\n**(d) Access.**\u2014An agency shall furnish records, data, and system access the Advocate requests under this section. This subsection does not authorize the Advocate to obtain information whose disclosure to the Advocate is prohibited by another provision of law.\n\n**(d-1) Withholding on a legal prohibition.**\u2014An agency withholding records under subsection (d) shall furnish the Advocate a written statement identifying the provision of law relied upon, describing the records withheld by category, and stating whether the prohibition would permit disclosure of the records in a form from which individually identifiable information has been removed. A statement that does not identify the provision, or that describes the records only as prohibited, does not satisfy this subsection. The Advocate shall publish each statement furnished under this subsection and, where none is furnished, the fact that records were withheld without one.\n\n**(d-2) Delinquency list.**\u2014The Advocate shall maintain and publish a list of agencies and covered processes for which a complete and timely entry has not been furnished under section 1-1(b). The Director of the Office of Management and Budget may rely on the list in administering section 1-3(a)."},{"anchor":"sec-1-3","heading":"\u00a7 1-3. Consequence of delinquency.","part":"SECTION 1 \u2014 THE GOVERNMENT BURDEN LEDGER","body":"**(a) Condition on new collections.**\u2014Beginning on the date set in the schedule, an agency that has not furnished a complete and timely entry under section 1-1(b) for a covered process may not obtain approval under section 3507 of title 44, United States Code, for a new or revised collection of information conducted in connection with that covered process, and any approval so obtained is of no effect.\n\n**(b) Exceptions.**\u2014Subsection (a) does not apply to a collection of information the agency head certifies is necessary to respond to a public health emergency, a natural disaster, or a threat to life, that is required by a statute enacted after the date of enactment of this Act, or that is a revision the Advocate has published as adopting a recommendation referred under section 4-1 or as reducing interaction burden under section 1-5(b). **These are the only exceptions to subsection (a). No other exception applies, whether by construction, by an agency's determination of good cause, or otherwise, and the omission of any further exception is a determination of Congress and not a gap.**\n\n**(b-1) Form of the certification.**\u2014A certification under subsection (b) shall be in writing, shall be published, and shall state the emergency, disaster, or threat relied upon and the connection between it and the collection of information. A certification that recites the words of subsection (b) without stating that connection does not satisfy this section, and the collection remains subject to subsection (a).\n\n**(c) Service unaffected.**\u2014Subsection (a) does not authorize an agency to suspend, delay, or decline to administer a covered process, and an agency's delinquency under section 1-1(b) is not a ground for reducing service under that process."},{"anchor":"sec-1-4","heading":"\u00a7 1-4. The unit of measurement.","part":"SECTION 1 \u2014 THE GOVERNMENT BURDEN LEDGER","body":"**(a) Prescribed by the Advocate.**\u2014The Advocate shall prescribe and publish the unit of measurement.\n\n**(b) Prospective change only.**\u2014A change to the unit of measurement applies prospectively only. The Advocate shall publish, with each change, the reason for it and a restatement of the two most recently published years under both the prior and the revised unit.\n\n**(c) Agency may not define.**\u2014An agency may not define, for purposes of the Ledger, what constitutes one covered process, one interaction, one step, or one document.\n\n**(d) Interim methodology.**\u2014Until the Advocate first publishes a unit of measurement for a class of covered processes, an agency shall furnish the figures required by section 1-1(b) using the methodology it uses to prepare estimates under section 3506(c)(1)(A)(iv) of title 44, United States Code, and shall publish the methodology it used. The duty to furnish under section 1-1(b) does not depend on the Advocate having prescribed a unit of measurement, and is not suspended by the absence of one."},{"anchor":"sec-1-5","heading":"\u00a7 1-5. Comparison and credit.","part":"SECTION 1 \u2014 THE GOVERNMENT BURDEN LEDGER","body":"**(a) Comparison.**\u2014The Advocate shall publish comparisons of covered processes within classes the Advocate defines as comparable. A comparison shall be of covered processes and shall not rank agencies in the aggregate.\n\n**(b) Credit.**\u2014The Advocate shall publish annually a statement identifying each agency that reduced an interaction burden without being required to do so, the covered process concerned, and the change in the published figures on which the identification rests. The Advocate shall not identify an agency under this subsection except by reference to a change in figures published in the Ledger."},{"anchor":"sec-1-6","heading":"\u00a7 1-6. Persons who do not appear in the Ledger.","part":"SECTION 1 \u2014 THE GOVERNMENT BURDEN LEDGER","body":"The Advocate shall publish annually a statement describing the categories of persons whose interaction burden the Ledger does not measure, including persons who did not begin a covered process, and shall state what would be required to measure it."},{"anchor":"sec-2-1","heading":"\u00a7 2-1. The channel.","part":"SECTION 2 \u2014 THE FEEDBACK CHANNELS","body":"**(a) Establishment.**\u2014Beginning on the date set in the schedule, each agency shall provide, for each covered process, a means by which a person may report a difficulty encountered in that process. The means shall be available\u2014\n\n**(1)** on each form the covered process requires, in the medium of that form;\n\n**(2)** on each website and digital service through which the covered process may be conducted, on the page where the person is asked to act;\n\n**(3)** at each office at which the covered process may be conducted in person; and\n\n**(4)** through the telephone channel, if any, through which the covered process may be conducted.\n\n**(b) No condition on filing.**\u2014A person may file a report under this section\u2014\n\n**(1)** without identifying themselves;\n\n**(2)** whether or not the person completed the covered process;\n\n**(3)** whether or not the person began the covered process; and\n\n**(4)** without first seeking assistance, reconsideration, or review from the agency.\n\n**(c) What may be reported.**\u2014A report may state any difficulty the person encountered, including that a requirement was unclear, that a document demanded was difficult or impossible to obtain, that the person waited longer than expected, that the person was asked for information the person had already supplied, that the person could not reach a person authorized to act, and that the person gave up. This subsection describes and does not limit what may be reported.\n\n**(d) Burden on the person.**\u2014A report under this section shall be capable of being filed in a single step and shall not require the person to supply information about themselves, to create an account, or to accept terms of use. An agency does not satisfy this section by providing a means that requires any of those things, or by providing a general contact address not specific to the covered process.\n\n**(e) Routing.**\u2014Each agency shall transmit reports filed under this section to the Advocate as received. The Advocate shall establish and publish the form of transmission. Until the Advocate does so, an agency shall transmit reports in the form the agency holds them, and the duty to transmit is not suspended by the absence of a published form.","partHeads":[{"heading":"SECTION 2 \u2014 THE FEEDBACK CHANNELS","body":""}]},{"anchor":"sec-2-2","heading":"\u00a7 2-2. The administrator channel.","part":"SECTION 2 \u2014 THE FEEDBACK CHANNELS","body":"**(a) Who may report.**\u2014An officer or employee of an agency, of a State, local, or Tribal government, or of a contractor, subcontractor, grantee, or subgrantee, who performs or supervises work in a covered process, may report to the Advocate a difficulty that the covered process imposes on a person or on the person performing the work.\n\n**(b) Voluntary.**\u2014A report under this subsection is voluntary. Nothing in this Act requires any person to make one, and no duty to report is created by this section.\n\n**(c) Protection; Federal employees.**\u2014A personnel action, as defined in section 2302(a)(2)(A) of title 5, United States Code, taken or not taken, or threatened to be taken or not taken, with respect to an employee or applicant because of a report made under this section **shall be treated as a prohibited personnel practice described in section 2302(b)(8) of title 5, United States Code**, and every provision of law governing the investigation, adjudication, and remedy of such a practice applies to it. This subsection applies whether or not the report evidences a violation of law, rule, or regulation, gross mismanagement, a gross waste of funds, an abuse of authority, or a substantial and specific danger to public health or safety, and whether or not the report concerns a policy decision that lawfully exercises discretionary authority.\n\n**(c-1) Protection; employees of contractors, grantees, and subgrantees.**\u2014A discharge, demotion, or other discrimination against an employee of a contractor, subcontractor, grantee, subgrantee, or personal services contractor because of a report made under this section **shall be treated as a reprisal prohibited by section 4712(a)(1) of title 41, United States Code**, and every provision of that section governing complaint, investigation, and remedy applies to it. This subsection applies whether or not the report evidences a matter described in that section.\n\n**(c-2) The Advocate is a protected recipient.**\u2014For purposes of subsections (c) and (c-1), a report made to the Advocate is a disclosure to a person authorized to receive it. The Advocate is not required to be designated by the head of any agency, and no such designation is a condition of the protection this section confers.\n\n**(c-3) Existing protections preserved.**\u2014Nothing in this section narrows, conditions, or supersedes a protection available under section 2302 of title 5, United States Code, section 4712 of title 41, United States Code, or any other provision of law. A person who is protected by another provision of law remains protected by it, and this section is available in addition to and not instead of it.\n\n**(c-4) Reach, stated rather than implied.**\u2014This section reaches an employee or applicant to whom section 2302 of title 5, United States Code, applies, and an employee to whom section 4712 of title 41, United States Code, applies. An officer or employee of a State, local, or Tribal government to whom neither section applies is not reached by subsections (c) and (c-1), and the omission is a determination of Congress made in recognition of the limits stated in section 0-4(c), and not a gap.\n\n**(d) No duty on another government.**\u2014This section confers a protection on an officer or employee of a State, local, or Tribal government and imposes no obligation on that government. A State, local, or Tribal government is not required to permit, facilitate, or refrain from any act by reason of this section, except that this section is not a ground for any adverse action described in subsection (c)."},{"anchor":"sec-2-3","heading":"\u00a7 2-3. Reports by others.","part":"SECTION 2 \u2014 THE FEEDBACK CHANNELS","body":"A person assisting another person in a covered process, including a person providing legal assistance, a navigator, an enrollment assister, and an organization serving persons who use the covered process, may file a report under section 2-1 on the basis of difficulties encountered by persons that person assists."},{"anchor":"sec-2-4","heading":"\u00a7 2-4. Publication.","part":"SECTION 2 \u2014 THE FEEDBACK CHANNELS","body":"**(a) Duty.**\u2014The Advocate shall publish reports received under this Section, in structured and machine-readable form, not less frequently than the interval set in the schedule.\n\n**(b) De-identification.**\u2014Before publication, the Advocate shall remove information that identifies or can reasonably be used to identify a person, by expert determination. The Advocate shall obtain and publish the determination, including the qualifications of the person making it and the methods applied. Publication of a report in a form from which such information has not been removed does not satisfy subsection (a) and is prohibited.\n\n**(b-1) Structured fields published first.**\u2014Where a report consists of both structured responses and text supplied by the person, the Advocate shall publish the structured responses within the interval required by subsection (a), and shall publish the text when the determination required by subsection (b) has been made for it. The difficulty of de-identifying text is not a ground for withholding the structured responses, and the Advocate shall publish the number of reports whose text is awaiting that determination.\n\n**(c) Aggregation.**\u2014The Advocate shall publish, for each covered process, the number of reports received, the difficulties most frequently reported, and whether the agency has stated that it has acted on them.\n\n**(d) Nothing turns on volume.**\u2014No duty under this Act arises, and no consequence under this Act follows, from the number of reports received concerning a covered process. The number is published as information and is not a threshold, a trigger, or a measure of merit."},{"anchor":"sec-2-5","heading":"\u00a7 2-5. Use of reports.","part":"SECTION 2 \u2014 THE FEEDBACK CHANNELS","body":"**(a) One-way rule.**\u2014A report filed under this Section, and any information in it\u2014\n\n**(1)** may not be used as a basis for any adverse action against the person who filed it or the person the report concerns;\n\n**(2)** may not be used in determining that person's eligibility for, or entitlement to, any benefit, service, status, license, permit, registration, or authorization;\n\n**(3)** is not admissible against that person in any proceeding; and\n\n**(4)** may be used to grant, restore, expedite, or increase a benefit, service, or status, or to correct an error, where the information supports doing so.\n\n**(b) No adverse use against the program.**\u2014A report filed under this Section, and any publication under section 2-4, may not be the basis for an agency action reducing, restricting, or eliminating a covered process, a benefit, or a program. Where an agency or the Office of Management and Budget relies on a report or publication under this Section in support of a recommendation to Congress that a covered process, benefit, or program be reduced, restricted, or eliminated, the agency or the Office shall publish, with that recommendation, the complete Ledger entry for the covered process, including the figures required by section 1-1(b)(5), (6), and (9). This subsection does not limit the use of a report or publication to simplify a covered process without reducing any benefit, service, status, right, or protection, and nothing in this subsection restricts what any officer may communicate to Congress.\n\n**(c) Closed list.**\u2014The uses permitted by this section are those and only those it provides. A use omitted from this section is a determination of Congress and is not a gap."},{"anchor":"sec-2-6","heading":"\u00a7 2-6. Amendment to the Paperwork Reduction Act.","part":"SECTION 2 \u2014 THE FEEDBACK CHANNELS","body":"**The amendment.**\u2014Section 3518(c) of title 44, United States Code, is amended\u2014\n\n**(1)** in paragraph (1)\u2014\n\n**(A)** in subparagraph (C), by striking \"or\" at the end;\n\n**(B)** in subparagraph (D), by striking the period at the end and inserting a semicolon; and\n\n**(C)** by adding at the end the following new subparagraphs:\n\n**(E)** by an agency from persons who use, or who have sought to use, a service, benefit, or process the agency provides, concerning the burden, usability, accessibility, or clarity of that service, benefit, or process, where\u2014\n\n**(i)** a response is voluntary;\n\n**(ii)** no benefit, service, status, or obligation of the respondent turns on whether the respondent responds or on what the respondent says; and\n\n**(iii)** the agency does not use the information collected to make a determination concerning any respondent; or\n\n**(F)** in the course of testing the usability of a paper or digital form, a website, or a service that an agency provides or is developing, by an officer, employee, or contractor of the agency\u2014\n\n**(i)** observing, in person or remotely, any number of individuals using or attempting to use the form, website, or service; or\n\n**(ii)** asking any number of individuals so observed structured, semi-structured, or open-ended questions concerning their use of the form, website, or service, or the burden it imposes on them,\n\nwhere each condition in paragraph (3) is met.\n\n**(2)** by adding at the end the following new paragraph:\n\n**(3)** The conditions referred to in paragraph (1)(F) are that\u2014\n\n**(A)** each participant has agreed, before the testing begins, to take part in it, and participation is voluntary;\n\n**(B)** no benefit, service, status, or obligation of a participant turns on whether the participant takes part or on what the participant says or does, other than a payment for taking part in an amount fixed before the testing begins that does not vary with what the participant says or does;\n\n**(C)** the agency does not use the information obtained, or disclose it to any person for use, to make a determination concerning, or as the basis for any adverse action against, any participant;\n\n**(D)** the agency does not collect or maintain information identifying a participant, other than information needed to contact the participant and a recording of the testing made and retained only for the purpose of improving the form, website, or service tested; and\n\n**(E)** the agency does not use the information obtained to make causal inferences in a way that implies statistical rigor, and does not publish descriptive statistics derived from it.\n\n**As amended, section 3518(c) will read as follows** \u2014 new language marked as inserted, struck language marked. *This display is illustrative; the operative amendment is stated in paragraphs (1) and (2) above.*\n\n> **(c)(1)** Except as provided in paragraph (2), this subchapter shall not apply to the collection of information\u2014\n>\n> **(A)** during the conduct of a Federal criminal investigation or prosecution, or during the disposition of a particular criminal matter;\n>\n> **(B)** during the conduct of\u2014 **(i)** a civil action to which the United States or any official or agency thereof is a party; or **(ii)** an administrative action or investigation involving an agency against specific individuals or entities;\n>\n> **(C)** by compulsory process pursuant to the Antitrust Civil Process Act and section 13 of the Federal Trade Commission Improvements Act of 1980; ~~or~~\n>\n> **(D)** during the conduct of intelligence activities as defined in section 3.4(e) of Executive Order No. 12333, issued December 4, 1981, or successor orders, or during the conduct of cryptologic activities that are communications security activities~~.~~ **;**\n>\n> **(E) by an agency from persons who use, or who have sought to use, a service, benefit, or process the agency provides, concerning the burden, usability, accessibility, or clarity of that service, benefit, or process, where\u2014**\n>\n> **(i) a response is voluntary;**\n>\n> **(ii) no benefit, service, status, or obligation of the respondent turns on whether the respondent responds or on what the respondent says; and**\n>\n> **(iii) the agency does not use the information collected to make a determination concerning any respondent; or**\n>\n> **(F) in the course of testing the usability of a paper or digital form, a website, or a service that an agency provides or is developing, by an officer, employee, or contractor of the agency\u2014**\n>\n> **(i) observing, in person or remotely, any number of individuals using or attempting to use the form, website, or service; or**\n>\n> **(ii) asking any number of individuals so observed structured, semi-structured, or open-ended questions concerning their use of the form, website, or service, or the burden it imposes on them,**\n>\n> **where each condition in paragraph (3) is met.**\n>\n> **(2)** This subchapter applies to the collection of information during the conduct of general investigations (other than information collected in an antitrust investigation to the extent provided in subparagraph (C) of paragraph (1)) undertaken with reference to a category of individuals or entities such as a class of licensees or an entire industry.\n>\n> **(3) The conditions referred to in paragraph (1)(F) are that\u2014**\n>\n> **(A) each participant has agreed, before the testing begins, to take part in it, and participation is voluntary;**\n>\n> **(B) no benefit, service, status, or obligation of a participant turns on whether the participant takes part or on what the participant says or does, other than a payment for taking part in an amount fixed before the testing begins that does not vary with what the participant says or does;**\n>\n> **(C) the agency does not use the information obtained, or disclose it to any person for use, to make a determination concerning, or as the basis for any adverse action against, any participant;**\n>\n> **(D) the agency does not collect or maintain information identifying a participant, other than information needed to contact the participant and a recording of the testing made and retained only for the purpose of improving the form, website, or service tested; and**\n>\n> **(E) the agency does not use the information obtained to make causal inferences in a way that implies statistical rigor, and does not publish descriptive statistics derived from it.**\n\n**(b) Construction.**\u2014The exclusions added by this section do not authorize an agency to obtain information for a purpose other than the purpose stated in subparagraph (E) or (F) of section 3518(c)(1) of title 44, United States Code, and information obtained under either subparagraph may not be used to make a determination concerning any respondent or participant. An agency that uses information so obtained to make such a determination has conducted a collection of information to which subchapter I of chapter 35 of that title applies. Subparagraph (F) reaches the observation and the questions it describes and not the information a participant submits through the form, website, or service tested, and a form, website, or service that is itself a collection of information remains subject to that subchapter. Testing described in subparagraph (F), and a solicitation described in subparagraph (E), is not a general investigation within the meaning of section 3518(c)(2) of that title.\n\n**(c) Existing exclusions preserved.**\u2014Nothing in this section narrows any exclusion from the definition of *\"information\"* in the regulations of the Director under chapter 35 of title 44, United States Code, and the omission of any such exclusion from section 3518(c) of that title is not a determination that it is unlawful."},{"anchor":"sec-3-1","heading":"\u00a7 3-1. Establishment; the Advocate.","part":"SECTION 3 \u2014 THE OFFICE OF THE PUBLIC ADVOCATE FOR GOVERNMENT SIMPLIFICATION","body":"**(a) Establishment.**\u2014There is established in the executive branch, as an independent establishment, the Office of the Public Advocate for Government Simplification.\n\n**(b) The Advocate.**\u2014The Office is headed by the Public Advocate for Government Simplification, appointed by the President, by and with the advice and consent of the Senate.\n\n**(c) Removal.**\u2014The Advocate serves at the pleasure of the President. This Act confers no protection against removal.\n\n**(d) Qualification; revolving door.**\u2014An individual may be appointed as Advocate only if the individual\u2014\n\n**(1)** was not, during the 2-year period ending with the appointment, an officer or employee of the Office of Management and Budget or of any other office within the Executive Office of the President; and\n\n**(2)** agrees, as a condition of appointment, not to accept employment with any agency, other than the Office, or employment by a contractor of an agency in the performance of that contract, during the 5-year period beginning on the date on which the individual ceases to serve as Advocate.\n\n**(e) Staff.**\u2014The Advocate shall appoint the officers and employees of the Office and shall supervise them. An officer or employee of the Office may not be detailed or assigned to another agency, and an officer or employee of another agency may not be detailed or assigned to the Office, except with the written consent of the Advocate.\n\n**(f) First assistant; vacancy.**\u2014\n\n**(1)** The Advocate shall designate in writing an officer of the Office appointed under subsection (e) as first assistant to the Advocate for purposes of section 3345 of title 5, United States Code, and shall publish the designation. Where no designation is in effect, the first assistant is the officer of the Office appointed under subsection (e) who has served longest in the Office.\n\n**(2)** Sections 3345 through 3349d of title 5, United States Code, govern the performance of the functions and duties of the Advocate during a vacancy in the position of Advocate.\n\n**(3)** A vacancy in the position of Advocate suspends no duty, no deadline, and no publication under this Act.\n\n**(g) Delegation.**\u2014The Advocate may delegate any function or duty this Act assigns to the Advocate or to the Office to an officer or employee of the Office, other than the delivery required by section 3-5(c) and the designation under subsection (f)(1). A function or duty so delegable is not a function or duty required by statute to be performed only by the Advocate, and a delegation in effect when a vacancy arises continues in effect during the vacancy.","partHeads":[{"heading":"SECTION 3 \u2014 THE OFFICE OF THE PUBLIC ADVOCATE FOR GOVERNMENT SIMPLIFICATION","body":""}]},{"anchor":"sec-3-2","heading":"\u00a7 3-2. Functions.","part":"SECTION 3 \u2014 THE OFFICE OF THE PUBLIC ADVOCATE FOR GOVERNMENT SIMPLIFICATION","body":"**(a) Functions.**\u2014The Office\u2014\n\n**(1)** maintains and publishes the Ledger, prescribes the unit of measurement, and verifies the figures furnished for the Ledger, under Section 1;\n\n**(2)** receives, transmits, publishes, and analyzes reports under Section 2;\n\n**(3)** refers recommendations to agencies, and publishes their responses and the record of them, under Section 4;\n\n**(4)** assembles, publishes, and transmits the annual Simplification Package under Section 5;\n\n**(5)** reports to Congress under section 3-5;\n\n**(6)** publishes the methods by which it measures, verifies, and compares interaction burden, in a form that a State, local, or Tribal government may adopt for its own processes; and\n\n**(7)** performs the further functions this Act assigns to it.\n\n**(b) Coverage does not depend on designation.**\u2014Whether a process is a covered process is determined by section 0-3(b). A designation, or the absence of a designation, of a program as a high impact service provider under section 321(5) of title 5, United States Code, does not affect whether a process is a covered process, and does not affect any duty under this Act. The Advocate may use such a designation to order the sequence in which the Office verifies covered processes and for no other purpose.\n\n**(c) No casework.**\u2014The Office does not act on behalf of any person in a matter pending before an agency and does not intervene in the disposition of any person's submission. A person who contacts the Office concerning a pending matter shall be informed of any office established by law to assist persons in matters before that agency, including the Office of the Taxpayer Advocate under section 7803(c) of the Internal Revenue Code of 1986 and the Citizenship and Immigration Services Ombudsman under section 452 of the Homeland Security Act of 2002."},{"anchor":"sec-3-3","heading":"\u00a7 3-3. No executive power.","part":"SECTION 3 \u2014 THE OFFICE OF THE PUBLIC ADVOCATE FOR GOVERNMENT SIMPLIFICATION","body":"**(a) What the Office does.**\u2014The Office receives, publishes, measures, verifies, compares, and refers. It performs the ministerial functions this Act assigns to it.\n\n**(b) What the Office does not do.**\u2014The Office shall not\u2014\n\n**(1)** issue an order directing an agency or a person to take or to refrain from any action;\n\n**(2)** adjudicate any claim, or determine the rights, eligibility, or liability of any person;\n\n**(3)** approve, disapprove, delay, suspend, or condition any collection of information, rule, guidance, or agency action;\n\n**(4)** impose any sanction, penalty, or charge; or\n\n**(5)** bring, intervene in, or appear as a party in any action in any court.\n\n**(c) Effect of a recommendation.**\u2014A recommendation of the Advocate has no legal effect except as Sections 4 and 5 provide. It does not bind any agency, does not confer any right, and is not agency action for purposes of chapter 7 of title 5, United States Code.\n\n**(d) Publication is not an order.**\u2014A publication under this Act, including the delinquency list under section 1-2(d-2), a corrected figure under section 1-2(c), a finding under section 4-3 that a response is non-responsive, and the unit of measurement under section 1-4, is a publication and not an order. The unit of measurement governs the computation of figures for the Ledger and governs nothing else.\n\n**(d-1) Reply.**\u2014An agency may transmit to the Advocate a written statement disputing a figure, finding, or comparison published under this Act concerning that agency, and the Advocate shall publish the statement with the figure, finding, or comparison it disputes. A statement under this subsection does not suspend, qualify, or delay any publication, duty, default, or consequence under this Act.\n\n**(e) Construction.**\u2014The Office has the functions this Act assigns to it and no others. This Section is a limit on the Office and not a limit on any other officer, and nothing in this Act shall be construed to confer on the Office any part of the executive power of the United States beyond the functions this Act assigns."},{"anchor":"sec-3-4","heading":"\u00a7 3-4. Independence by function.","part":"SECTION 3 \u2014 THE OFFICE OF THE PUBLIC ADVOCATE FOR GOVERNMENT SIMPLIFICATION","body":"**(a) Local offices.**\u2014Beginning on the date set in the schedule, the Advocate shall maintain at least one local office in each State, in the District of Columbia, in the Commonwealth of Puerto Rico, and in each territory or possession of the United States in which an agency conducts a covered process in person. Each local office\u2014\n\n**(1)** shall have a telephone number, an electronic address, and a postal address separate from those of every other agency;\n\n**(2)** shall receive reports under Section 2 in person and by each means section 2-1(a) requires;\n\n**(3)** shall inform each person at the person's first contact with the office that the Office is not part of the agency with which the person is dealing, does not act on the person's behalf before that agency, and reports directly to Congress; and\n\n**(4)** may decline to disclose to any agency that a person contacted the office, or the information the person provided.\n\n**(b) Identity of a person reporting under section 2-2.**\u2014The Office shall not disclose to any agency, or to any officer or employee of an agency, the identity of a person who made a report under section 2-2, or information from which that identity can reasonably be inferred, except with the written consent of that person given after the report was made. That consent is the only exception to this subsection, and the omission of any other exception is a determination of Congress and not a gap. A record described in this subsection is exempt from disclosure under section 552 of title 5, United States Code, and this subsection is a statute described in section 552(b)(3) of that title and specifically cites that section.\n\n**(c) Communication with Congress.**\u2014The Advocate may communicate with any committee or Member of Congress, and may respond to any request of a committee of Congress for information or testimony, without the prior review, comment, or amendment of any other officer or employee of the United States. This subsection is in addition to section 7211 of title 5, United States Code, and to section 3-5(c).\n\n**(d) Budget estimates.**\u2014The Advocate shall prepare the annual budget estimate of the Office and shall transmit it to the President. The President shall include in the budget transmitted under section 1105 of title 31, United States Code, the estimate as the Advocate transmitted it, without change, together with the President's own recommendation for the Office, stated separately. The Advocate shall transmit the estimate to the Committees on Appropriations of the House of Representatives and the Senate on the day it is transmitted to the President.\n\n**(e) Coordination with the service delivery chassis.**\u2014The Advocate shall coordinate with the Federal Government Service Delivery Lead under section 322 of title 5, United States Code. Nothing in this Act transfers to the Office a function of that Lead or of a Lead Agency Service Delivery Official under section 323 of that title, and nothing in this Act transfers to either of them a function of the Office.\n\n**(f) Agency point of contact.**\u2014The head of each agency may designate an officer of the agency to furnish entries under section 1-1(b), to transmit reports under section 2-1(e), and to respond under Section 4. Where no designation is in effect, the Lead Agency Service Delivery Official designated or appointed under section 323(b) of title 5, United States Code, performs those functions. A designation or its absence does not affect any duty of the agency under this Act."},{"anchor":"sec-3-5","heading":"\u00a7 3-5. Annual report; recommendations to Congress.","part":"SECTION 3 \u2014 THE OFFICE OF THE PUBLIC ADVOCATE FOR GOVERNMENT SIMPLIFICATION","body":"**(a) Report.**\u2014Not later than December 31 of each year, the Advocate shall deliver a report on the preceding fiscal year, which shall contain\u2014\n\n**(1)** a summary of the Ledger, including each covered process for which an entry was not furnished, was incomplete, or was corrected under section 1-2(c);\n\n**(2)** the number and categories of reports received under Section 2, and the difficulties most frequently reported;\n\n**(3)** each recommendation referred under Section 4 during the year or pending at its close, the agency to which it was referred, the date of referral, and whether the agency adopted it, declined it with reasons, was found non-responsive, or did not respond;\n\n**(4)** for each recommendation on which the agency has not taken the action recommended, the period that has elapsed since referral, the reasons the agency stated, and the name and position of the official of the agency who holds the authority to take that action;\n\n**(5)** each agency credited under section 1-5(b) and each comparison published under section 1-5(a);\n\n**(6)** the statement required by section 1-6;\n\n**(7)** the delinquency list under section 1-2(d-2) and each statement of withholding under section 1-2(d-1);\n\n**(8)** the status of each Simplification Package transmitted under Section 5 and not enacted;\n\n**(9)** the Office's own performance against each duty this Act imposes on it, including each publication made after the interval this Act requires, **and, for each local office under section 3-4(a), the reports received in person, the hours the office was open, and the time to reach a person authorized to act, at the median and at the ninetieth percentile**; and\n\n**(10)** recommendations for legislation.\n\n**(b) Recommendations to Congress at any time.**\u2014The Advocate may deliver a recommendation for legislation at any time, and shall deliver the Simplification Package under Section 5.\n\n**(c) Delivery without prior review.**\u2014Each report under subsection (a), each recommendation under subsection (b), and each Simplification Package shall be provided directly to the Committee on Oversight and Accountability of the House of Representatives, the Committee on Homeland Security and Governmental Affairs of the Senate, or the committee of that House that has succeeded to its jurisdiction, and each committee of the House of Representatives or the Senate with jurisdiction over an agency to which a recommendation in it is addressed, without any prior review, comment, or amendment by the President, the Director of the Office of Management and Budget, or any other officer or employee of any agency. The Advocate shall publish each report, recommendation, and package on the day it is delivered.\n\n**(d) Identification of an official.**\u2014An official identified under subsection (a)(4) is the official who holds the authority, by statute, regulation, or delegation, to take the recommended action, and where that authority is held by the head of the agency, the head of the agency. Where the authority has been delegated, the report identifies both the official to whom it is delegated and the official from whom the delegation runs. Where the agency has not, in its response under Section 4 or otherwise in writing, identified the official who holds the authority, the report identifies the head of the agency.\n\n**(e) The President's own views.**\u2014Nothing in this section restricts the President, the Director of the Office of Management and Budget, or the head of any agency from transmitting to Congress, at any time, that officer's own views on a report, recommendation, or package delivered under this section."},{"anchor":"sec-3-6","heading":"\u00a7 3-6. Continuity.","part":"SECTION 3 \u2014 THE OFFICE OF THE PUBLIC ADVOCATE FOR GOVERNMENT SIMPLIFICATION","body":"**(a) Activities that continue.**\u2014The functions of the Office under Sections 1, 2, 4, and 5 and under section 3-5, and the furnishing of entries and transmission of reports by agencies under sections 1-1(b) and 2-1(e), are activities authorized by law to continue during a lapse in appropriations. The employment of personal services for those activities is authorized by law for purposes of section 1342 of title 31, United States Code, and an obligation incurred for them before an appropriation is made is authorized by law for purposes of section 1341(a)(1)(B) of that title.\n\n**(b) Excepted personnel.**\u2014The officers and employees necessary to perform the activities in subsection (a) shall be designated as excepted from furlough during a lapse in appropriations. The Advocate, and the head of each agency as to the activities of that agency, shall identify those positions in the shutdown plan of the Office or the agency, publish the identification, and maintain staffing sufficient to perform the activities without interruption.\n\n**(c) No suspension.**\u2014A lapse in appropriations, a failure to request an appropriation, and a failure to apportion, allot, or obligate amounts appropriated suspend no duty, no deadline, no default, and no publication under this Act, and none is a defense in an action to enforce a duty this Act makes ministerial."},{"anchor":"sec-3-7","heading":"\u00a7 3-7. Transition.","part":"SECTION 3 \u2014 THE OFFICE OF THE PUBLIC ADVOCATE FOR GOVERNMENT SIMPLIFICATION","body":"**(a) Before the first Advocate.**\u2014Until the first Advocate is appointed under section 3-1(b), each function and duty this Act assigns to the Office or to the Advocate, other than the delivery required by section 3-5(c), is performed by the Federal Government Service Delivery Lead under section 322 of title 5, United States Code, and section 3-3 applies to that official in performing it. A figure furnished under section 1-1(b) and a report transmitted under section 2-1(e) during that period shall be published as furnished or transmitted.\n\n**(b) End of transition.**\u2014Subsection (a) ceases to apply on the date on which the first Advocate is appointed. A unit of measurement, form of transmission, or other instrument prescribed under subsection (a) remains in effect until the Advocate prescribes otherwise."},{"anchor":"sec-4-1","heading":"\u00a7 4-1. Referral of a recommendation.","part":"SECTION 4 \u2014 THE ADMINISTRATIVE TRACK","body":"**(a) Referral.**\u2014The Advocate may refer to an agency a recommendation that the agency reduce an interaction burden in a covered process by a change in a form, an evidence requirement, a procedure, a rule, guidance, a practice, or a service delivery channel.\n\n**(b) Public predicate.**\u2014A referral shall identify the entry in the Ledger, the report or aggregation published under Section 2, the verification finding under section 1-2, or the comparison under section 1-5 on which it rests, and shall state the interaction burden the change would reduce. A recommendation that rests on none of those is not referred under this section, and the omission of any other predicate is a determination of Congress and not a gap.\n\n**(c) Form; publication.**\u2014A referral shall be in writing, shall state the change recommended with the particularity of a proposed amendment to the form, requirement, procedure, rule, guidance, or practice concerned, and shall be published on the day it is referred.\n\n**(d) Effect.**\u2014A referral is a recommendation within section 3-3(c). It does not require the agency to make the change, and it is not the notice required by section 553(b) of title 5, United States Code, except as section 4-4(c) provides.","partHeads":[{"heading":"SECTION 4 \u2014 THE ADMINISTRATIVE TRACK","body":""}]},{"anchor":"sec-4-2","heading":"\u00a7 4-2. The response.","part":"SECTION 4 \u2014 THE ADMINISTRATIVE TRACK","body":"**(a) Duty to respond.**\u2014Not later than the period set in the schedule after a referral, the agency shall deliver to the Advocate a written response that\u2014\n\n**(1)** adopts the recommendation, and states the date by which each step of the change will be made;\n\n**(2)** adopts the recommendation in part, states which part, states the date by which each step of that part will be made, and states specific reasons for not adopting the remainder; or\n\n**(3)** declines the recommendation and states specific reasons.\n\nThe agency may respond to related referrals in one response that addresses each.\n\n**(b) What does not satisfy subsection (a).**\u2014A response does not satisfy subsection (a) if it\u2014\n\n**(1)** states that the recommendation was considered, reviewed, or evaluated without stating a reason particular to it;\n\n**(2)** states a reason that would apply to any recommendation to reduce any interaction burden in any covered process;\n\n**(3)** asserts that a statute, regulation, or other requirement of law requires the burden without citing the provision and stating what it requires;\n\n**(4)** asserts a cost, a risk of fraud or error, or an effect on any other function without stating the basis for the assertion and its estimated magnitude;\n\n**(5)** states a date under paragraph (1) or (2) that is not a date certain, or that is later than the period set in the schedule after the response without stating specific reasons for the delay; or\n\n**(6)** does not identify, by name and position, the official of the agency who holds the authority to make the change recommended, and where that authority has been delegated, both the official to whom it is delegated and the official from whom the delegation runs.\n\n**(c) Discretion as to outcome is not discretion as to response.**\u2014Whether to adopt a recommendation is committed to the agency. Whether to respond, whether to respond within the period, and whether to state reasons that satisfy subsection (b) are not, and the duty to respond is ministerial.\n\n**(d) Publication.**\u2014The Advocate shall publish each response on the day it is received."},{"anchor":"sec-4-3","heading":"\u00a7 4-3. Non-responsive response.","part":"SECTION 4 \u2014 THE ADMINISTRATIVE TRACK","body":"**(a) Finding.**\u2014Where a response does not satisfy section 4-2(b), the Advocate may publish a finding that it does not, stating the paragraph of section 4-2(b) that the response fails and how.\n\n**(b) Cure.**\u2014The agency may deliver a further response within the period set in the schedule after a finding is published. A further response that satisfies section 4-2 is the agency's response for purposes of this Section. A statement transmitted under section 3-3(d-1) is published with the finding and is not a further response.\n\n**(c) Effect.**\u2014A response the subject of a finding under subsection (a), for which no further response satisfying section 4-2 is delivered within the period set in subsection (b), is treated for purposes of section 4-4 and section 4-5 as no response."},{"anchor":"sec-4-4","heading":"\u00a7 4-4. Docketing by operation of law.","part":"SECTION 4 \u2014 THE ADMINISTRATIVE TRACK","body":"**(a) Docketing.**\u2014Where the agency has not delivered a response satisfying section 4-2 by the end of the period set in section 4-2(a), or where section 4-3(c) applies, the recommendation is docketed by operation of this subsection, and no further act by any person is required for it to be docketed.\n\n**(b) Publication for comment.**\u2014Not later than 15 days after a recommendation is docketed, the Advocate shall transmit it to the Office of the Federal Register, and the Office of the Federal Register shall publish it in the Federal Register, under the name of the agency, as a recommended change on which the agency has not responded, with an invitation to the public to comment within the period set in the schedule. The notice shall contain the terms of the change as stated in the referral, the legal authority under which the agency could make it as the Advocate identifies that authority, and the matter described in section 553(b)(1) through (4) of title 5, United States Code, as the Advocate states it. A document published under this subsection is a document required to be published by Act of Congress within the meaning of section 1505(a)(3) of title 44, United States Code.\n\n**(c) Comment satisfies notice for adoption.**\u2014Where the agency adopts by a rule the change as stated in the referral, or with modifications that are a logical outgrowth of the publication under subsection (b) and the comments received under it, the publication under subsection (b) shall be treated as the notice required by section 553(b) of title 5, United States Code, for that rule, and the comments received shall be treated as the participation required by section 553(c) of that title. This subsection applies to no other rule, and does not satisfy a notice, comment, or procedural requirement imposed on the rule by any other provision of law.\n\n**(d) Response after comment.**\u2014Not later than the period set in the schedule after the close of the comment period, the agency shall deliver a response satisfying section 4-2 that also addresses the comments received. Section 4-3 applies to that response. A recommendation on which no such response is delivered remains docketed and is reported under section 3-5(a)(4) and section 4-5 until the agency responds or makes the change.\n\n**(e) One-way.**\u2014Docketing under this section adopts nothing, amends no form, requirement, procedure, rule, or guidance, confers no authority on the Advocate, and imposes no obligation on any person other than the agency's duties under this Section. It does not satisfy any requirement of law for the making of a rule other than as subsection (c) provides."},{"anchor":"sec-4-5","heading":"\u00a7 4-5. The scorecard.","part":"SECTION 4 \u2014 THE ADMINISTRATIVE TRACK","body":"**(a) Publication.**\u2014The Advocate shall publish, for each agency, not less frequently than the interval set in the schedule, in machine-readable form\u2014\n\n**(1)** the number of recommendations referred, and for each, its date of referral and its predicate under section 4-1(b);\n\n**(2)** the number adopted, adopted in part, declined, found non-responsive under section 4-3, docketed under section 4-4, and awaiting response, and the median number of days between referral and a response satisfying section 4-2;\n\n**(3)** for each recommendation adopted or adopted in part, whether the change has been made, as verified by the Advocate under section 1-2, and where it has not, the number of days elapsed since the date the agency stated under section 4-2(a)(1) or (2);\n\n**(4)** for each recommendation declined, docketed, or overdue, the name and position of the official identified under section 4-2(b)(6), or, where none was identified, the head of the agency; and\n\n**(5)** each agency credited under section 1-5(b) for a change made without a referral; and\n\n**(6)** the number of directions received by the agency under section 4-6(a), the number honored, and the number to which a determination under section 4-6(b) was applied, by covered process.\n\n**(b) Adoption is the change, not the statement.**\u2014A recommendation is recorded as implemented under subsection (a)(3) only when the Advocate has verified that the change has been made in the covered process. A statement that the recommendation has been adopted is recorded as a statement."},{"anchor":"sec-4-6","heading":"\u00a7 4-6. Acceptance of a fact verified by another agency.","part":"SECTION 4 \u2014 THE ADMINISTRATIVE TRACK","body":"**(a) Acceptance on the person's direction.**\u2014Beginning on the date set in the schedule, where an agency has verified a fact concerning a person in a covered process, and the person directs in writing that the verification be furnished to another agency for a covered process of that agency\u2014\n\n**(1)** the verifying agency shall furnish to the receiving agency a statement of the fact verified, the date of verification, and the standard or method applied, and shall not furnish the documents or records on which the verification rested unless the person's direction so states; and\n\n**(2)** the receiving agency shall accept the statement as establishing that fact for the covered process, and shall not require the person to establish the fact again, except as subsection (b) provides.\n\n**(a-1) Form of the statement.**\u2014The Advocate shall publish the form of the statement under subsection (a)(1). Until the Advocate does so, a verifying agency furnishes the statement in any writing that contains the matters subsection (a)(1) requires, and the duty to furnish is not suspended by the absence of a published form.\n\n**(b) Published determination.**\u2014Subsection (a)(2) does not apply where, before the person's direction, the receiving agency has published, for that covered process, a determination identifying the fact, the standard the covered process requires for it, and the reason the standard applied by the verifying agency does not meet it. A determination that does not identify a standard, or that states a reason applicable to verifications generally, does not satisfy this subsection.\n\n**(c) Requirements of statute.**\u2014Subsection (a) does not satisfy a requirement, imposed by a statute, that a specified document be presented or that a specified agency perform the verification. The omission of any further exception is a determination of Congress and not a gap.\n\n**(d) Establishing only.**\u2014Subsection (a) applies to establishing a fact in the person's favor. It does not supply a fact for use in denying, reducing, suspending, or terminating a benefit, service, status, license, permit, registration, or authorization, and a verification furnished under subsection (a) may not be so used.\n\n**(e) Income; the Keep Your Benefits Act.**\u2014Verification of income is governed by the Keep Your Benefits Act, and this section does not modify that Act, section 7-3 of that Act, or any requirement that Act names. Where that Act is not in effect, this subsection refers to it as it read on the date of enactment of this Act.\n\n**(f) No acquisition; no linked record.**\u2014Nothing in this section authorizes an agency to obtain, purchase, or retain information concerning a person other than the statement furnished under subsection (a)(1) on the person's direction. No agency shall create or retain, by reason of this section, a record linking individually identifiable information across programs or agencies other than the record of the transmission itself. A direction under subsection (a) is the prior written consent of the person for purposes of section 552a(b) of title 5, United States Code, as to the statement furnished. The Data Warrant Act governs every acquisition of information this section does not describe."},{"anchor":"sec-4-7","heading":"\u00a7 4-7. Attestation.","part":"SECTION 4 \u2014 THE ADMINISTRATIVE TRACK","body":"**(a) Authority.**\u2014An agency may accept, in place of a document a covered process requires, the person's written declaration of the fact the document would establish, subscribed as true under penalty of perjury in the form provided by section 1746 of title 28, United States Code, where no statute requires the document.\n\n**(b) Reasons for declining.**\u2014Where a referral under section 4-1 recommends that an agency accept a declaration under subsection (a) for a covered process, a response declining it shall state the risk of fraud or error relied on, the basis for the estimate of that risk, and the interaction burden the document requirement imposes as stated in the Ledger. A response that does not is a response described in section 4-2(b)(4).\n\n**(c) Verification preserved.**\u2014Nothing in this section prevents an agency from verifying a fact declared under subsection (a) by any means otherwise lawful, and nothing in this section alters the consequence under any provision of law of a false declaration."},{"anchor":"sec-4-8","heading":"\u00a7 4-8. Amendment to the Paperwork Reduction Act \u2014 the renewal review.","part":"SECTION 4 \u2014 THE ADMINISTRATIVE TRACK","body":"**The amendment.**\u2014Section 3507(h)(1) of title 44, United States Code, is amended\u2014\n\n**(1)** in subparagraph (A), by striking \"; and\" at the end and inserting a semicolon;\n\n**(2)** in subparagraph (B), by striking the period at the end and inserting \"; and\"; and\n\n**(3)** by adding at the end the following new subparagraph:\n\n**(C)** for a collection of information conducted in connection with a covered process, as that term is defined in section 0-3 of the Government Simplification Act, include in the review under subparagraph (A) and in the submission under subparagraph (B)\u2014\n\n**(i)** the entry for that covered process in the Government Burden Ledger published under section 1-1 of that Act, and any corrected figure published under section 1-2(c) of that Act;\n\n**(ii)** the aggregation published for that covered process under section 2-4(c) of that Act; and\n\n**(iii)** for each recommendation referred under section 4-1 of that Act concerning the collection of information, whether the agency adopted it, and, where it did not, the reasons stated in the agency's response under section 4-2 of that Act.\n\n**As amended, section 3507(h)(1) will read as follows** \u2014 new language marked as inserted, struck language marked. *This display is illustrative; the operative amendment is stated in paragraphs (1) through (3) above.*\n\n> **(h)(1)** If an agency decides to seek extension of the Director's approval granted for a currently approved collection of information, the agency shall\u2014\n>\n> **(A)** conduct the review established under section 3506(c), including the seeking of comment from the public on the continued need for, and burden imposed by the collection of information; ~~and~~\n>\n> **(B)** after having made a reasonable effort to seek public comment, but no later than 60 days before the expiration date of the control number assigned by the Director for the currently approved collection of information, submit the collection of information for review and approval under this section, which shall include an explanation of how the agency has used the information that it has collected~~.~~**; and**\n>\n> **(C) for a collection of information conducted in connection with a covered process, as that term is defined in section 0-3 of the Government Simplification Act, include in the review under subparagraph (A) and in the submission under subparagraph (B)\u2014**\n>\n> **(i) the entry for that covered process in the Government Burden Ledger published under section 1-1 of that Act, and any corrected figure published under section 1-2(c) of that Act;**\n>\n> **(ii) the aggregation published for that covered process under section 2-4(c) of that Act; and**\n>\n> **(iii) for each recommendation referred under section 4-1 of that Act concerning the collection of information, whether the agency adopted it, and, where it did not, the reasons stated in the agency's response under section 4-2 of that Act.**\n\n**(b) Construction.**\u2014Subparagraph (C) of section 3507(h)(1) of title 44, United States Code, as added by this section, requires the agency to include the matters it describes. It does not confer on the Director any authority the Director does not otherwise have, does not make the Ledger, an aggregation, or a recommendation binding on the Director, and does not condition the approval of any collection of information on any matter other than those on which section 3508 of that title conditions it."},{"anchor":"sec-4-9","heading":"\u00a7 4-9. Eligibility of a business concern established from records.","part":"SECTION 4 \u2014 THE ADMINISTRATIVE TRACK","body":"**(a) Definitions.**\u2014In this section:\n\n**(1) Administrator.**\u2014The term *\"Administrator\"* means the Administrator of the Small Business Administration.\n\n**(2) Covered award.**\u2014The term *\"covered award\"* means a contract, subcontract, cooperative agreement, cooperative research and development agreement, or grant described in section 3(w)(1) of the Small Business Act (15 U.S.C. 632(w)(1)).\n\n**(3) Covered financing.**\u2014The term *\"covered financing\"* means a loan made, or a guarantee of a loan made, under section 7(a), 7(b), or 7(m) of the Small Business Act (15 U.S.C. 636(a), (b), (m)) or a loan made under title V of the Small Business Investment Act of 1958 (15 U.S.C. 695 et seq.) and a debenture guaranteed under that title to fund such a loan, including a loan or guarantee under any program established by a provision of law enacted after the date of enactment of this Act that is carried out under any of those provisions, unless that provision of law refers to this section and states that this section does not apply.\n\n**(4) Size element.**\u2014The term *\"size element\"* means the gross receipts or the number of employees by which a size standard established under section 3(a)(2) of the Small Business Act (15 U.S.C. 632(a)(2)) measures a business concern.\n\n**(5) Record element.**\u2014The term *\"record element\"* means an element of eligibility for, or of the amount of, a covered financing that is stated in terms of an applicant's receipts, payroll, income, or number of employees.\n\n**(6) Comparison.**\u2014The term *\"comparison\"* means a comparison, made under this section, of a figure a business concern represents with the corresponding figure in the return information of that concern.\n\n**(b) Size, from records.**\u2014Beginning on the date set in the schedule, the Administrator shall make a comparison of the concern's own figure for each size element that a business concern states under section 3(x)(3) of the Small Business Act (15 U.S.C. 632(x)(3)) in a certification under section 3(x)(1) of that Act or in a registration described in section 3(w)(2)(C) of that Act, not later than the period set in the schedule after the certification or registration. The comparison is of the concern's own return information, and does not establish or address whether the concern is affiliated with any other concern.\n\n**(c) Financing, from records.**\u2014Beginning on the date set in the schedule, before making or guaranteeing a covered financing, the Administrator shall\u2014\n\n**(1)** make a comparison of each record element the applicant represents; and\n\n**(2)** review each database described in section 3354(a)(2) of title 31, United States Code. For a covered financing, the words *\"as appropriate\"* in that section do not permit the omission of any database it names, and section 3354(b)(4) of that title continues to apply.\n\n**(d) The source; consent.**\u2014\n\n**(1)** A comparison under this section is made from return information that the business concern has consented to disclose to the Administrator under section 6103(c) of the Internal Revenue Code of 1986, and the Administrator shall provide, with each form on which a certification, registration, or application described in subsection (b) or (c) is made, the means to give that consent in the same step.\n\n**(1A)** Not later than the period set in the schedule after a request by the Administrator on a consent described in paragraph (1), the Secretary of the Treasury shall disclose the return information consented to. The Secretary may decline only on a determination under the second sentence of section 6103(c) of the Internal Revenue Code of 1986, made in writing for a class of cases and published, and a comparison not made by reason of such a determination is a comparison to which subsection (g) applies.\n\n**(2)** A concern that does not consent may instead furnish the Administrator with copies of the returns concerned, and the Administrator makes the comparison from them. A concern's decision not to consent is not a ground for any adverse action and is not considered in any determination.\n\n**(3)** Information obtained under this subsection may be used only to make the comparison for which it was obtained. No person may create or retain, by reason of this section, a record linking individually identifiable information across programs or agencies other than the record of the comparison and its result. This section authorizes the acquisition of no information other than as this subsection describes.\n\n**(e) A comparison that matches.**\u2014\n\n**(1) Effect.**\u2014Where a comparison matches, the element is established as to the concern's own receipts, payroll, income, or employees for the purpose for which it was represented, and the Administrator shall not require the concern to establish that element again by any document or record for that purpose during the period to which the certification, registration, or application relates.\n\n**(2) Tolerance.**\u2014A comparison matches where the figures differ by not more than the tolerance the Administrator publishes for that element, computed as the size standard or the program concerned computes the figure. A tolerance may not be less than the minimum or more than the maximum percentage set in the schedule, and until the Administrator publishes one for an element, the tolerance for that element is the interim percentage set in the schedule.\n\n**(3) Challenges preserved.**\u2014A match does not establish that a concern is not affiliated with another concern, does not bar a challenge by an interested party to the size or status of a concern under any other provision of law, and is not a defense to any such challenge.\n\n**(f) A comparison that does not match; cure.**\u2014\n\n**(1) Notice.**\u2014Where a comparison does not match, the Administrator shall, not later than the period set in the schedule after the comparison, notify the concern in writing, stating the element, the figure the concern represented, the figure in the source, the source and period compared, and the means by which the concern may respond.\n\n**(2) Cure.**\u2014The concern may respond, within the period set in the schedule after the notice, with a record, an explanation, or a corrected representation. A response may establish the element by any means that the law otherwise applicable to that element permits.\n\n**(3) Determination.**\u2014Not later than the period set in the schedule after the response, or after the period for response ends without one, the Administrator shall determine the element under the law otherwise applicable to it, on the record including the response.\n\n**(4) The non-match is not the determination.**\u2014A comparison that does not match is not a determination that a concern is ineligible, is not a ground for denying, reducing, or withdrawing any covered award or covered financing, and is not, by itself, a finding of misrepresentation for purposes of section 3(w) or 16(d) of the Small Business Act (15 U.S.C. 632(w), 645(d)). The Administrator may withhold a covered award or covered financing on the ground of an element compared under this section only after the notice under paragraph (1) has been given, and only until the determination under paragraph (3).\n\n**(g) The government's delay.**\u2014Where the Administrator has not completed a comparison within the period set in subsection (b) or in the schedule for subsection (c)(1), has not given notice within the period set for paragraph (1) of subsection (f), or has not made a determination within the period set for paragraph (3) of that subsection, the concern's representation of the element stands for the covered award or covered financing concerned as it would under the law otherwise applicable in the absence of this section, and no covered award or covered financing may be withheld by reason of this section. This subsection does not bar a later comparison, a later determination, or any remedy under any other provision of law for a representation that was false. This subsection does not apply to a comparison that could not be made because the concern neither consented under subsection (d)(1) nor furnished returns under subsection (d)(2); in that case the Administrator gives notice under subsection (f)(1) stating that fact, and paragraphs (2) through (4) of subsection (f) apply.\n\n**(h) Publication; the output standard.**\u2014The Administrator shall furnish to the Advocate, and the Advocate shall publish with the Ledger, for each fiscal year and separately for covered awards and covered financings\u2014\n\n**(1)** the number of comparisons made, by element and source, and the number that matched;\n\n**(2)** the number of notices under subsection (f)(1), the number of responses, the number of determinations that the element was established on the response, and the number of determinations that it was not;\n\n**(3)** the median and the ninetieth-percentile time from certification, registration, or application to comparison, and from response to determination; and\n\n**(4)** the number of instances in which subsection (g) applied, by the period missed.\n\nA number reported under paragraph (4) that exceeds the level set in the schedule is evidence of inadequate staffing or systems, and the Advocate shall state it as such in the report under section 3-5. For each fiscal year in which it does, the Administrator shall publish, and furnish to the Advocate for that report, the number of positions and the amount required to bring the number under that level, stating each separately. A determination that an element was established on the response is reported as a comparison that did not match an eligible concern, and the Advocate shall publish the rate of such determinations for each element and source.\n\n**(i) Existing duties preserved.**\u2014This section is in addition to section 3354 of title 31, United States Code, and to the duties of the Administrator under sections 8, 31, and 36 of the Small Business Act (15 U.S.C. 637, 657a, 657f). A comparison under subsection (b) that matches satisfies the duty of the Administrator under section 36(f)(2)(B)(ii)(I) of the Small Business Act (15 U.S.C. 657f(f)(2)(B)(ii)(I)) as to the size element compared.\n\n**(j) What this section does not do.**\u2014This section adds no element to the eligibility of any concern for any covered award or covered financing, changes no size standard, and alters no standard or burden of proof under which any element is determined. It does not modify the Keep Your Benefits Act. Sections 0-4(b), 6-1(d), and 7-2(c) do not bar the withholding subsection (f)(4) permits, the consent and furnishing described in subsection (d), the statement of figures required by section 3(x)(3) of the Small Business Act as added by section 8-6, or the response described in subsection (f)(2), each of which this section requires or permits by its own terms, and the withholding in subsection (f)(4) is the only withholding this section permits. Section 4-6(d) governs a statement furnished under section 4-6 and does not govern a comparison under this section."},{"anchor":"sec-5-1","heading":"\u00a7 5-1. The Simplification Package.","part":"SECTION 5 \u2014 THE CONGRESSIONAL TRACK","body":"**(a) Transmittal.**\u2014Not later than March 1 of each year, the Advocate shall transmit to Congress, under section 3-5(c), a Simplification Package for that year.\n\n**(b) Contents.**\u2014The Package consists of the text of a bill, and each provision in it\u2014\n\n**(1)** amends or repeals a provision of Federal law that requires, or is the stated reason for, an interaction burden in a covered process;\n\n**(2)** is a burden reduction within the meaning of section 6-1 and does nothing section 6-1 excludes;\n\n**(3)** does not increase any person's liability for any tax, and does not raise revenue;\n\n**(4)** does not appropriate funds; and\n\n**(5)** rests on a public predicate under subsection (c).\n\n**(c) Public predicate.**\u2014A provision may be included in the Package only if the Package identifies, for that provision, the entry in the Ledger, the report or aggregation published under Section 2, the verification finding under section 1-2, the comparison under section 1-5, or the response under section 4-2 in which an agency stated that the burden is required by the provision of law to be amended, on which the provision rests. A provision that rests on none of those may not be included, and the omission of any other predicate is a determination of Congress and not a gap.\n\n**(d) Draft published for comment.**\u2014Not later than 90 days before transmittal, the Advocate shall publish the draft Package and shall receive public comment on it for not less than 45 days. The Package as transmitted shall be accompanied by a statement identifying each change made after publication of the draft and the comments to which each responds, and each provision in the draft that was omitted and the reason.\n\n**(e) Statements accompanying the Package.**\u2014The Package shall be accompanied by\u2014\n\n**(1)** for each provision, a statement of the interaction burden it reduces, by reference to the figures in the Ledger, and the covered processes affected;\n\n**(2)** for each provision amending the Internal Revenue Code of 1986 or affecting the administration of any tax, a statement that the provision does not increase any person's liability for any tax, the basis for that statement, and the expected effect of the provision on revenue and on tax administration, with the method by which the effect was estimated; and\n\n**(3)** a statement that each provision satisfies subsection (b), stating for each the paragraph of section 6-1 under which it is a burden reduction.\n\n**(f) Severability of provisions.**\u2014Each provision of the Package shall be drafted so that it operates independently of every other provision of the Package.\n\n**(g) Effect.**\u2014The Package is a recommendation within section 3-3(c). No provision of the Package has any effect except by enactment.","partHeads":[{"heading":"SECTION 5 \u2014 THE CONGRESSIONAL TRACK","body":""}]},{"anchor":"sec-5-2","heading":"\u00a7 5-2. Expedited consideration.","part":"SECTION 5 \u2014 THE CONGRESSIONAL TRACK","body":"**(a) Simplification bill.**\u2014For purposes of this section, the term *\"Simplification bill\"* means only a bill\u2014\n\n**(1)** introduced in a House of Congress within the 10-day period beginning on the date on which the Advocate transmits the Package under section 5-1(a) for a year;\n\n**(2)** the matter after the enacting clause of which consists solely of the text of the Package as transmitted, without change; and\n\n**(3)** the title of which is as follows: \"A bill to enact the Simplification Package for [year] transmitted under the Government Simplification Act,\" the blank being filled with the year.\n\n**(b) Referral.**\u2014A Simplification bill introduced in the House of Representatives shall be referred to the Committee on Oversight and Accountability of the House of Representatives, and a Simplification bill introduced in the Senate shall be referred to the Committee on Homeland Security and Governmental Affairs of the Senate, or in either case to the committee of that House that has succeeded to its jurisdiction, and to no other committee.\n\n**(c) Discharge.**\u2014If the committee to which a Simplification bill is referred has not reported the bill (or an identical bill) by the end of the 20-day period beginning on the date on which the Package is transmitted, the committee shall be, at the end of that period, discharged from further consideration of the bill, and the bill shall be placed on the appropriate calendar of the House involved.\n\n**(d) Consideration.**\u2014\n\n**(1)** On or after the third day after the date on which the committee to which a Simplification bill is referred has reported, or has been discharged under subsection (c) from further consideration of, the bill, it is in order (even though a previous motion to the same effect has been disagreed to) for any Member of the respective House to move to proceed to the consideration of the bill. A Member may make the motion only on the day after the calendar day on which the Member announces to the House concerned the Member's intention to make the motion, except that, in the case of the House of Representatives, the motion may be made without such prior announcement if the motion is made by direction of the committee to which the bill was referred. All points of order against the bill (and against consideration of the bill) are waived, other than a point of order under subsection (h). The motion is highly privileged in the House of Representatives and is privileged in the Senate and is not debatable. The motion is not subject to amendment, or to a motion to postpone, or to a motion to proceed to the consideration of other business. A motion to reconsider the vote by which the motion is agreed to or disagreed to shall not be in order. If a motion to proceed to the consideration of the bill is agreed to, the respective House shall immediately proceed to consideration of the bill without intervening motion, order, or other business, and the bill shall remain the unfinished business of the respective House until disposed of.\n\n**(2)** Debate on the bill, and on all debatable motions and appeals in connection therewith, shall be limited to not more than 10 hours, which shall be divided equally between those favoring and those opposing the bill. An amendment to the bill is not in order. A motion further to limit debate is in order and not debatable. A motion to postpone, or a motion to proceed to the consideration of other business, or a motion to recommit the bill is not in order. A motion to reconsider the vote by which the bill is agreed to or disagreed to is not in order.\n\n**(3)** Immediately following the conclusion of the debate on a Simplification bill and a single quorum call at the conclusion of the debate if requested in accordance with the rules of the appropriate House, the vote on final passage of the bill shall occur.\n\n**(4)** Appeals from the decisions of the Chair relating to the application of the rules of the Senate or the House of Representatives, as the case may be, to the procedure relating to a Simplification bill shall be decided without debate.\n\n**(e) Consideration by the other House.**\u2014\n\n**(1)** If, before the passage by one House of a Simplification bill of that House, that House receives from the other House a Simplification bill, then the following procedures shall apply:\n\n**(A)** The bill of the other House shall not be referred to a committee and may not be considered in the House receiving it except in the case of final passage as provided in subparagraph (B)(ii).\n\n**(B)** With respect to a Simplification bill of the House receiving the bill\u2014\n\n**(i)** the procedure in that House shall be the same as if no bill had been received from the other House; but\n\n**(ii)** the vote on final passage shall be on the bill of the other House.\n\n**(2)** Upon disposition of the bill received from the other House, it shall no longer be in order to consider the bill that originated in the receiving House.\n\n**(3)** Where the Package contains a provision amending the Internal Revenue Code of 1986, the vote on final passage in the Senate shall be on the Simplification bill of the House of Representatives, and a Simplification bill of the Senate shall not be passed by the Senate.\n\n**(f) Period.**\u2014The procedure in subsections (c) through (e) applies to a Simplification bill only during the 90 session days beginning on the date on which the Package is transmitted. After that period a Simplification bill is considered under the rules of the House concerned as any other bill.\n\n**(g) Late or absent transmittal.**\u2014Where the Advocate has not transmitted the Package for a year by March 1 of that year, no bill is a Simplification bill for that year, and a Package transmitted after that date is considered under section 3-5 as a recommendation for legislation and not under this section.\n\n**(h) Point of order against a provision outside section 5-1(b).**\u2014\n\n**(1)** It shall not be in order in either House to consider a Simplification bill that contains a provision that does not satisfy section 5-1(b). A point of order under this paragraph may be raised by any Member.\n\n**(2)** Where a point of order under paragraph (1) is sustained, the bill is not in order, and the Advocate may, within 10 days after the point of order is sustained, transmit a revised Package omitting the provision and making no other change. A bill introduced within 10 days after that transmittal, the matter after the enacting clause of which consists solely of the text of the revised Package, is the Simplification bill for that year, and the periods in subsections (c) and (f) run from the revised transmittal.\n\n**(3)** The Chair's ruling on a point of order under paragraph (1) shall be made on the basis of the Package, the statements accompanying it under section 5-1(e), and section 6-1, and an appeal from the ruling shall be decided without debate.\n\n**(i) No inference.**\u2014If Congress does not enact a Simplification bill, no court or agency may infer any intent of Congress from any action or inaction of Congress with regard to the Package, any provision in it, any related statute, or the bill.\n\n**(j) Rules of the Senate and House.**\u2014This section is enacted by Congress\u2014\n\n**(1)** as an exercise of the rulemaking power of the Senate and House of Representatives, respectively, and as such it is deemed a part of the rules of each House, respectively, but applicable only with respect to the procedure to be followed in that House in the case of a Simplification bill, and it supersedes other rules only to the extent that it is inconsistent with such rules; and\n\n**(2)** with full recognition of the constitutional right of either House to change the rules (so far as relating to the procedure of that House) at any time, in the same manner, and to the same extent as in the case of any other rule of that House."},{"anchor":"sec-5-3","heading":"\u00a7 5-3. Report on disposition.","part":"SECTION 5 \u2014 THE CONGRESSIONAL TRACK","body":"The Advocate shall publish, not later than 30 days after the end of the period in section 5-2(f) for a year, a statement identifying each provision of the Package for that year and whether it was enacted, and, where the Package was not enacted, the stage at which consideration ended in each House. The statement is included in the report under section 3-5(a)(8)."},{"anchor":"sec-6-1","heading":"\u00a7 6-1. Burden reduction.","part":"SECTION 6 \u2014 PROTECTIONS","body":"**(a) What is a burden reduction.**\u2014A change to a form, requirement, procedure, rule, guidance, practice, service delivery channel, or provision of law is a burden reduction if it does one or more of the following and does nothing subsection (b) describes:\n\n**(1)** removes or reduces a requirement that a person supply information, a document, a record, or a signature;\n\n**(2)** removes or reduces a requirement that a person appear, wait, or make a submission through a particular service delivery channel, or adds a channel through which a person may act;\n\n**(3)** shortens the time between a person's submission and the agency's final action, or establishes or shortens a period within which the agency must act;\n\n**(4)** removes a requirement that a person supply information the agency or another agency already holds, or that a fact already verified be verified again;\n\n**(5)** replaces a requirement that a person supply a document with a declaration by the person or with a verification the agency performs from records it holds;\n\n**(6)** extends a period within which a person must act, or removes a deadline the lapse of which causes a procedural denial;\n\n**(7)** requires or permits an agency to correct its own error without a further submission by the person;\n\n**(8)** simplifies the language, length, or structure of a form, notice, or requirement without changing what a person must establish; or\n\n**(9)** otherwise reduces interaction burden as measured under the unit of measurement.\n\n**(b) What is never a burden reduction.**\u2014A change is not a burden reduction, whatever else it does, if it\u2014\n\n**(1)** reduces, conditions, or narrows any person's eligibility for, or the amount, duration, or scope of, a benefit, service, status, license, permit, registration, or authorization;\n\n**(2)** reduces, conditions, or narrows any right, remedy, or protection of any person, including a protection of health, safety, the environment, civil rights, consumers, or workers, or a duty owed to a person by an agency or by another person;\n\n**(3)** reduces the notice, hearing, appeal, or review to which a person is entitled, or shortens the period within which a person may seek it;\n\n**(4)** increases what a person must do, pay, or establish, including any tax, fee, or charge;\n\n**(5)** transfers an interaction burden from an agency to a person, or from the Federal Government to a State, local, or Tribal government without that government's written consent;\n\n**(6)** reduces, suspends, restricts, or eliminates a covered process, a program, a service, or the personnel or funds available for one; or\n\n**(7)** reduces the protection of a person's information under any provision of law.\n\n**(c) Subsection (b) governs.**\u2014A change described in both subsection (a) and subsection (b) is not a burden reduction. A change is assessed as a whole, and a burden reduction may not be combined with a change subsection (b) describes so as to bring the combination within subsection (a).\n\n**(d) This section governs every action under this Act.**\u2014A recommendation under section 4-1, a provision of a Package under section 5-1, a credit under section 1-5(b), and every other action this Act authorizes or requires, may be a burden reduction only as this section provides. Nothing in this Act authorizes any change subsection (b) describes, and a change subsection (b) describes made in reliance on this Act is not authorized by it, except as section 4-9(j) provides.","partHeads":[{"heading":"SECTION 6 \u2014 PROTECTIONS","body":""}]},{"anchor":"sec-6-2","heading":"\u00a7 6-2. Anti-substitution.","part":"SECTION 6 \u2014 PROTECTIONS","body":"**(a) No degradation in answer to a finding.**\u2014An agency shall not, in response to a referral under section 4-1, a figure published in the Ledger, a report or aggregation published under Section 2, a comparison under section 1-5, or an entry in the scorecard under section 4-5, reduce, suspend, restrict, or degrade a covered process, benefit, or service, or move persons from a measured service delivery channel to one that is not measured.\n\n**(b) Condition on a reduction.**\u2014An agency may reduce, suspend, restrict, or degrade a covered process, benefit, or service, or the personnel or funds available for one, during the period set in the schedule after a referral, publication, comparison, or scorecard entry concerning that covered process, only after publishing a statement that identifies the reduction, identifies each referral, publication, comparison, or scorecard entry concerning that covered process within that period, states the authority under which the reduction is made, and states the reason the reduction is not a response to any of them. A statement that does not identify each item, or that states a reason applicable to any reduction, does not satisfy this subsection.\n\n**(c) Regardless of form.**\u2014Subsections (a) and (b) apply to an action having substantially the same effect as a reduction, suspension, restriction, or degradation, regardless of form, title, or characterization, including a reassignment of personnel, a change in hours or locations, a change in the channels through which the covered process may be conducted, and a change in the order or priority in which submissions are acted on.\n\n**(d) Publication.**\u2014The Advocate shall publish each statement under subsection (b) with the Ledger entry for the covered process, and shall publish the fact where a reduction described in subsection (b) is made without one."},{"anchor":"sec-6-3","heading":"\u00a7 6-3. Use of information.","part":"SECTION 6 \u2014 PROTECTIONS","body":"**(a) Ledger and verification records.**\u2014Information furnished for the Ledger under section 1-1(b), data underlying it preserved under section 1-1(c-1), a record examined under section 1-2, and a statement furnished under section 4-6(a)(1) may not be used as a basis for any adverse action against any person, may not be used to determine any person's eligibility for or entitlement to any benefit, service, status, license, permit, registration, or authorization, and are not admissible against any person in any proceeding.\n\n**(b) Reports.**\u2014Section 2-5 governs reports filed under Section 2. This section is in addition to it.\n\n**(c) Closed list.**\u2014The uses this Act permits of information described in subsection (a) are those and only those this Act provides. A use omitted is a determination of Congress and not a gap."},{"anchor":"sec-6-4","heading":"\u00a7 6-4. Relation to regulatory budgeting; scope of this Act.","part":"SECTION 6 \u2014 PROTECTIONS","body":"**(a) No offset requirement.**\u2014Nothing in this Act requires or authorizes an agency to rescind, repeal, or refrain from adopting any rule, guidance, or requirement as a condition of adopting, retaining, or amending another, or to offset the cost of any rule, guidance, or requirement against the cost of another.\n\n**(b) The measure.**\u2014A reduction in interaction burden under this Act is measured under the unit of measurement. It is not measured by the number of rules, pages, words, sections, restrictions, or requirements, and a change in any of those is not a burden reduction by reason of that change.\n\n**(c) Standards unchanged.**\u2014Nothing in this Act alters the standard under which any rule is adopted, reviewed, or set aside, or the weight to be given any cost or benefit under any provision of law or any Executive order.\n\n**(d) Compliance cost not reached.**\u2014This Act reaches interaction burden as defined in section 0-3(d). It does not reach the cost of complying with a substantive requirement of a rule \u2014 what a rule requires a person to do, as distinct from what it requires a person to demonstrate \u2014 and nothing in this Act authorizes a recommendation, a Package provision, or a credit on that ground.\n\n**(e) Executive orders.**\u2014A requirement imposed by an Executive order that a rule, guidance, or requirement be rescinded as a condition of adopting another does not apply to a burden reduction made under this Act, and a burden reduction is not an action to which such a requirement attaches."},{"anchor":"sec-7-1","heading":"\u00a7 7-1. Effective date.","part":"SECTION 7 \u2014 GENERAL PROVISIONS","body":"**(a) In general.**\u2014This Act takes effect on the date of enactment.\n\n**(b) Dated duties.**\u2014A duty this Act states to begin on a date set in the schedule begins on that date. A duty this Act states to run from a fiscal year applies to the first fiscal year beginning after the date of enactment and to each fiscal year after it.\n\n**(c) Amendments.**\u2014An amendment made by section 2-6, section 4-8, or Section 8 applies to a collection of information, certification, document, form, or duty arising on or after the date of enactment, and to an extension sought under section 3507(h) of title 44, United States Code, on or after that date.","partHeads":[{"heading":"SECTION 7 \u2014 GENERAL PROVISIONS","body":""}]},{"anchor":"sec-7-2","heading":"\u00a7 7-2. Relationship to other law.","part":"SECTION 7 \u2014 GENERAL PROVISIONS","body":"**(a) In addition.**\u2014The duties this Act imposes are in addition to those imposed by chapter 35 of title 44, United States Code, by subchapter II of chapter 3 of title 5, United States Code, by the Plain Writing Act of 2010, and by the 21st Century Integrated Digital Experience Act. Nothing in this Act narrows any of them, and where a provision of this Act and a provision of any of them could both be complied with, both apply.\n\n**(b) Cross-references.**\u2014Where this Act refers to another Act of Congress, and that Act is not in effect, the reference is to that Act as it read on the date of enactment of this Act. This Act operates whether or not any other Act it refers to is in effect.\n\n**(c) Rules of construction stated once.**\u2014Section 0-4 applies to every Section of this Act, and section 6-1 governs every action taken under it."},{"anchor":"sec-7-3","heading":"\u00a7 7-3. Ministerial duties.","part":"SECTION 7 \u2014 GENERAL PROVISIONS","body":"**(a) Every publication and delivery.**\u2014Each publication, transmission, and delivery this Act requires of the Office or of the Advocate, and each publication this Act requires of an agency, is a ministerial duty. Section 1-1(d) applies to each of them as it applies to the Ledger, and a person entitled under section 1-1(d)(1) to the information a publication would contain may bring an action under section 1-1(d)(4) to compel it.\n\n**(b) Every response and statement.**\u2014The duty of an agency to respond under section 4-2 and section 4-4(d), to deliver a statement under section 1-2(d-1), section 6-2(b), or section 8-2, to furnish an entry under section 1-1(b) or a statement under section 4-6(a)(1), and of the Administrator to make a comparison under section 4-9(b) or (c)(1), to give notice under section 4-9(f)(1), to make a determination under section 4-9(f)(3), and to furnish the figures required by section 4-9(h), is a ministerial duty within the meaning of subsection (a), and the fact of non-performance is published by the Advocate as this Act provides.\n\n**(c) Stated expressly.**\u2014Where a duty under this Act requires no exercise of judgment as to whether to act, it is ministerial, and no inference to the contrary is drawn from the absence of that word in the provision creating it."},{"anchor":"sec-7-4","heading":"\u00a7 7-4. Resources and continuity of operations.","part":"SECTION 7 \u2014 GENERAL PROVISIONS","body":"**(a) Permanent appropriation for the Office.**\u2014There are appropriated to the Office, out of any money in the Treasury not otherwise appropriated, for each fiscal year beginning with the first fiscal year after the date of enactment, an amount not less than the annual amount set in the schedule, adjusted for each fiscal year after the first by the index set in the schedule. Amounts appropriated by this subsection are available without further appropriation and without fiscal year limitation, remain available until expended, and do not lapse. The amount set in the schedule is a floor and not a ceiling, and nothing in this subsection limits an appropriation for the Office in any other Act.\n\n**(b) Permanent appropriation for agency duties.**\u2014There are appropriated, out of any money in the Treasury not otherwise appropriated, such sums as are necessary for each agency to furnish the entries required by section 1-1(b), to provide the means required by section 2-1(a), to transmit reports under section 2-1(e), to respond under Section 4, to furnish statements under section 4-6(a)(1), and for the Administrator of the Small Business Administration to make comparisons, give notices, make determinations, and furnish figures under section 4-9, including the personnel who do so, available without fiscal year limitation. Amounts under this subsection are appropriated permanently and do not lapse.\n\n**(c) No obligation without appropriation \u2014 and what it does not reach.**\u2014No duty imposed by this Act on an agency, other than a duty funded by subsection (b) or made ministerial by section 7-3, is effective in a fiscal year except to the extent that amounts are appropriated to carry it out; where amounts are insufficient, the agency shall publish a statement identifying each duty not performed and the amount required to perform it, and shall transmit it to the Advocate, who shall publish it with the Ledger entry. **No duty funded by subsection (a) or (b), and no duty section 7-3 makes ministerial, is excused, tolled, suspended, or rendered ineffective by the absence or insufficiency of an appropriation, by a failure to request one, or by a failure to apportion, allot, or obligate amounts appropriated, and a defense that the duty was not effective for want of funds is not available in any action to enforce it.**\n\n**(d) No displacement.**\u2014Amounts appropriated by this section are in addition to, and shall not be derived from, amounts otherwise available to any agency for any other purpose. No agency shall reduce, delay, or discontinue a function it performed before the date of enactment in order to carry out this Act, and a reduction so made is a reduction described in section 6-2.\n\n**(e) Continuity.**\u2014Section 3-6 governs the Office and the agency activities it names during a lapse in appropriations.\n\n**(f) Annual certification of adequacy.**\u2014The Advocate shall certify annually, in the report under section 3-5, whether the amounts available to the Office are sufficient to perform each duty this Act imposes on it, and shall state, where they are not, the duty affected, the amount required, and the consequence of the shortfall."},{"anchor":"sec-7-5","heading":"\u00a7 7-5. Severability.","part":"SECTION 7 \u2014 GENERAL PROVISIONS","body":"**(a) In general.**\u2014If any provision of this Act, or its application to any person or circumstance, is held invalid, the remainder of this Act and its application to other persons or circumstances are not affected. Each Section, and each section within a Section, is severable from every other.\n\n**(b) No scheme; construction against aggregation.**\u2014Each duty this Act imposes is a discrete rule of measurement, publication, response, or protection, stands independently of every other, and is complete in itself. Congress would have enacted each standing alone, in the absence of every other, and directs that each be so construed and, if necessary, so preserved. No provision shall be construed as part of a larger scheme by reason of its enactment in the same statute as any other provision, and a holding that any provision or group of provisions is invalid shall not be extended to any other provision on the ground that the provisions together constitute a scheme, a design, or a pattern.\n\n**(c) The Office's structure from the Act's substance.**\u2014The provisions of Section 3 describing the establishment, appointment, and structure of the Office are severable from every duty this Act imposes on an agency. If any provision of Section 3 is held invalid, the duties of Sections 1, 2, 4, and 6 remain in force, the entries and reports they require are published by the Federal Government Service Delivery Lead under section 3-7 as though no Advocate had been appointed, and section 1-1(d) applies to that publication.\n\n**(d) The enforcement provisions from the duties.**\u2014Section 1-1(d) and section 7-3 are severable from every duty they enforce. If an action under section 1-1(d) is held not to present a case or controversy, or is held unavailable to any class of person, the duty to publish remains a ministerial duty of the Office, section 3-5(a)(9) reports its non-performance, and every other provision of this Act remains in force.\n\n**(e) The congressional track.**\u2014Section 5 is severable from every other Section, and section 5-2 is severable from section 5-1. If section 5-2 is held invalid, or is superseded by a rule of either House, section 5-1 remains in force and the Package is delivered under section 3-5 as a recommendation for legislation.\n\n**(f) The person-directed verification.**\u2014Section 4-6 is severable from every other provision. If section 4-6 is held invalid in whole or as to any class of fact, agency, or person, every other provision of Section 4 remains in force.\n\n**(f-1) Eligibility from records.**\u2014Section 4-9 is severable from every other provision, and each of subsections (b), (c)(1), and (c)(2) of that section is severable from the others. If subsection (f)(4) or (g) of that section is held invalid, no covered award or covered financing may be withheld by reason of that section.\n\n**(g) The amendments.**\u2014Each amendment made by section 2-6, section 4-8, and Section 8 is severable from every other and from the freestanding provisions of this Act. If any amendment is held invalid, the provision of this Act to which it relates remains in force as an enactment of this Act, and the host statute reads as it read before the amendment.\n\n**(h) Applications to State, local, and Tribal officers.**\u2014The application of any protection under this Act to an officer or employee of a State, local, or Tribal government is severable from its application to every other person. If any such application is held invalid, the protection remains in force as to every officer and employee of an agency and of a contractor, subcontractor, grantee, or subgrantee."},{"anchor":"sec-7-6","heading":"\u00a7 7-6. Definitions for this Section and the schedule.","part":"SECTION 7 \u2014 GENERAL PROVISIONS","body":"**(a) Index.**\u2014The term *\"index\"* means the measure set in the schedule by which the amount in section 7-4(a) is adjusted, and a reference to the index is to the measure as published by the agency that publishes it, without adjustment by any other officer.\n\n**(b) Day.**\u2014A reference in this Act to a number of days is to calendar days, except in Section 5, where a reference to session days is to session days of the House concerned and a reference to days without qualification is to calendar days."},{"anchor":"schedule","heading":"SCHEDULE","part":null,"body":"| Provision | Value | Note |\n|---|---|---|\n| \u00a7 0-3(b) | *(Reserved.)* | Annual interaction threshold at which a process becomes a covered process |\n| \u00a7 0-3(e)(2) | *(Reserved.)* | Period of inaction after which a begun process is abandoned |\n| \u00a7 1-1(d)(7) | *(Reserved.)* | Limitations period for an action to compel publication |\n| \u00a7 1-1(e-1) | *(Reserved.)* | Transition period after which an element may no longer be reported unavailable |\n| \u00a7 1-2(b) | *(Reserved.)* | Maximum interval between verifications of a covered process |\n| \u00a7 1-3(a) | *(Reserved.)* | Onset date for the delinquency consequence |\n| \u00a7 2-1(a) | *(Reserved.)* | Onset date by which the channel must be available on each covered process |\n| \u00a7 2-4(a) | *(Reserved.)* | Maximum interval between publications of received reports |\n| \u00a7 3-4(a) | *(Reserved.)* | Onset date by which the local-office floor must be met |\n| \u00a7 4-2(a) | *(Reserved.)* | Period after referral within which the agency must respond |\n| \u00a7 4-2(b)(5) | *(Reserved.)* | Period after a response beyond which a stated implementation date requires specific reasons for the delay |\n| \u00a7 4-3(b) | *(Reserved.)* | Period after a non-responsive finding within which the agency may cure |\n| \u00a7 4-4(b) | *(Reserved.)* | Public comment period on a docketed recommendation |\n| \u00a7 4-4(d) | *(Reserved.)* | Period after the close of comment within which the agency must respond |\n| \u00a7 4-5(a) | *(Reserved.)* | Maximum interval between scorecard publications |\n| \u00a7 4-6(a) | *(Reserved.)* | Onset date for acceptance of a verified fact on the person's direction |\n| \u00a7 4-9(b) | *(Reserved.)* | Onset date for the comparison of size elements, and the period after a certification or registration within which the comparison is made |\n| \u00a7 4-9(c) | *(Reserved.)* | Onset date for the comparison and database review before a covered financing, and the period after the application to the Administrator, or the lender's request for a guarantee, within which the comparison is made |\n| \u00a7 4-9(e)(2) | *(Reserved.)* | Minimum and maximum tolerance, as percentages, within which a comparison matches, and the interim tolerance, between them, applying until the Administrator publishes one |\n| \u00a7 4-9(d)(1A) | *(Reserved.)* | Period after the Administrator's request within which the Secretary of the Treasury discloses consented return information |\n| \u00a7 4-9(f)(1) | *(Reserved.)* | Period after a comparison within which notice of a non-match is given |\n| \u00a7 4-9(f)(2) | *(Reserved.)* | Period after notice within which the concern may respond |\n| \u00a7 4-9(f)(3) | *(Reserved.)* | Period after a response, or after the response period ends, within which the determination is made |\n| \u00a7 4-9(h) | *(Reserved.)* | Annual number of instances of government delay above which the count is stated as evidence of inadequate staffing or systems |\n| \u00a7 6-2(b) | *(Reserved.)* | Period after a referral, publication, comparison, or scorecard entry during which a reduction requires the published statement |\n| \u00a7 7-4(a) | *(Reserved.)* | Annual floor of the permanent appropriation for the Office |\n| \u00a7 7-4(a) | *(Reserved.)* | Index by which the floor is adjusted annually |"},{"anchor":"sec-8-1","heading":"\u00a7 8-1. Amendments to the Government Service Delivery Improvement Act.","part":"SECTION 8 \u2014 AMENDMENTS TO RELATED ACTS","body":"**(a) Correcting amendments.**\u2014Sections 322(a)(9), 322(a)(11), and 323(b)(7) of title 5, United States Code, are each amended by striking \"15 U.S.C. 3501 note\" and inserting \"44 U.S.C. 3501 note\".\n\n**(b) Section 322.**\u2014Section 322(a) of title 5, United States Code, is amended\u2014\n\n**(1)** in paragraph (9), by inserting \"and the Government Simplification Act\" after \"Public Law 115\u2013336)\";\n\n**(2)** in paragraph (10), by striking \"delivery.\" and inserting \"delivery, and with the Public Advocate for Government Simplification as section 3-4(e) of the Government Simplification Act provides.\"; and\n\n**(3)** by adding at the end the following:\n\n**(12)** Furnish to the Public Advocate for Government Simplification, on request, the standards, policies, guidelines, performance metrics, and data developed or collected under paragraphs (5) through (7), and account, in the standards, policies, and guidelines developed under paragraph (5), for the figures published in the Government Burden Ledger under section 1-1 of the Government Simplification Act.\n\n**(c) Section 323.**\u2014Section 323(b) of title 5, United States Code, is amended\u2014\n\n**(1)** in paragraph (7), by inserting \"and the Government Simplification Act\" after \"Public Law 115\u2013336)\"; and\n\n**(2)** by adding at the end the following:\n\n**(9)** Where no designation under section 3-4(f) of the Government Simplification Act is in effect, furnish the entries required by section 1-1(b) of that Act, transmit the reports required by section 2-1(e) of that Act, and respond under Section 4 of that Act, on behalf of the agency.\n\n**As amended, sections 322(a)(9)\u2013(12) and 323(b)(7)\u2013(9) will read as follows** \u2014 new language marked as inserted, struck language marked. *This display is illustrative; the operative amendments are stated in subsections (a) through (c).*\n\n> **\u00a7 322(a) \u2026**\n>\n> **(9)** Ensure agency service delivery initiatives, including those related to the 21st Century Integrated Digital Experience Act (~~15~~ **44** U.S.C. 3501 note; Public Law 115\u2013336) **and the Government Simplification Act**, are identified in agency congressional budget justifications.\n>\n> **(10)** Coordinate with Office of Management and Budget stakeholders and other agency stakeholders on Government service ~~delivery.~~ **delivery, and with the Public Advocate for Government Simplification as section 3-4(e) of the Government Simplification Act provides.**\n>\n> **(11)** In collaboration with relevant agency officials as appropriate, and in coordination with relevant Office of Management and Budget stakeholders, ensure that websites of agencies, including those associated with high impact service providers, are consistent with the objectives of this subchapter, the 21st Century Integrated Digital Experience Act (~~15~~ **44** U.S.C. 3501 note; Public Law 115\u2013336), and any other applicable law.\n>\n> **(12) Furnish to the Public Advocate for Government Simplification, on request, the standards, policies, guidelines, performance metrics, and data developed or collected under paragraphs (5) through (7), and account, in the standards, policies, and guidelines developed under paragraph (5), for the figures published in the Government Burden Ledger under section 1-1 of the Government Simplification Act.**\n>\n> **\u00a7 323(b) \u2026**\n>\n> **(7)** Assist with the implementation by the agency of the 21st Century Integrated Digital Experience Act (~~15~~ **44** U.S.C. 3501 note; Public Law 115\u2013336) **and the Government Simplification Act**.\n>\n> **(8)** Assist in the incorporation of the Government service delivery requirements established under this subchapter in agency plans (such as strategic plans or annual performance plans).\n>\n> **(9) Where no designation under section 3-4(f) of the Government Simplification Act is in effect, furnish the entries required by section 1-1(b) of that Act, transmit the reports required by section 2-1(e) of that Act, and respond under Section 4 of that Act, on behalf of the agency.**\n\n**(d) Construction.**\u2014Paragraph (12) of section 322(a) of title 5, United States Code, as added by this section, requires the Lead to account for the Ledger's figures in developing standards. It does not make the Ledger, or any figure in it, a standard, and does not confer on the Director or the Lead any authority over the Ledger, the unit of measurement, or the Office.","partHeads":[{"heading":"SECTION 8 \u2014 AMENDMENTS TO RELATED ACTS","body":""}]},{"anchor":"sec-8-2","heading":"\u00a7 8-2. Amendment to the Paperwork Reduction Act \u2014 the certifications.","part":"SECTION 8 \u2014 AMENDMENTS TO RELATED ACTS","body":"**The amendment.**\u2014Section 3506(c)(3) of title 44, United States Code, is amended by adding at the end the following flush sentences:\n\n\"For a collection of information conducted in connection with a covered process, as that term is defined in section 0-3 of the Government Simplification Act, the certification under this paragraph shall state the figures published for that covered process in the Government Burden Ledger under section 1-1 of that Act, shall state whether the Public Advocate for Government Simplification has published a corrected figure under section 1-2(c) of that Act and, if so, the corrected figure, and shall be furnished to the Public Advocate on submission to the Director. The Public Advocate shall verify, under section 1-2 of that Act, the estimate of burden on which the certification under subparagraph (C) rests and the statement under subparagraph (D), and shall publish the result. A certification that states a figure the Public Advocate has corrected without stating the corrected figure does not satisfy this paragraph.\"\n\n**As amended, the closing text of section 3506(c)(3) will read as follows** \u2014 new language marked as inserted. *This display is illustrative; the operative amendment is stated above.*\n\n> **(J)** to the maximum extent practicable, uses information technology to reduce burden and improve data quality, agency efficiency and responsiveness to the public; **For a collection of information conducted in connection with a covered process, as that term is defined in section 0-3 of the Government Simplification Act, the certification under this paragraph shall state the figures published for that covered process in the Government Burden Ledger under section 1-1 of that Act, shall state whether the Public Advocate for Government Simplification has published a corrected figure under section 1-2(c) of that Act and, if so, the corrected figure, and shall be furnished to the Public Advocate on submission to the Director. The Public Advocate shall verify, under section 1-2 of that Act, the estimate of burden on which the certification under subparagraph (C) rests and the statement under subparagraph (D), and shall publish the result. A certification that states a figure the Public Advocate has corrected without stating the corrected figure does not satisfy this paragraph.** and\n\n**(b) Construction.**\u2014The sentences added by this section require a certification to carry the Ledger's figures and require the Public Advocate to verify and publish. They do not condition the Director's approval of any collection of information on the Public Advocate's verification, do not authorize the Public Advocate to approve, disapprove, or delay any collection, and do not alter section 3508 of title 44, United States Code."},{"anchor":"sec-8-3","heading":"\u00a7 8-3. Conforming amendment to the Paperwork Reduction Act \u2014 the delinquency condition.","part":"SECTION 8 \u2014 AMENDMENTS TO RELATED ACTS","body":"**The amendment.**\u2014Section 3507(a) of title 44, United States Code, is amended\u2014\n\n**(1)** in paragraph (2), by striking \"and\" at the end;\n\n**(2)** in paragraph (3), by striking the period at the end and inserting \"; and\"; and\n\n**(3)** by adding at the end the following:\n\n**(4)** in the case of a collection of information conducted in connection with a covered process, as that term is defined in section 0-3 of the Government Simplification Act, the agency is not, as to that covered process, an agency described in section 1-3(a) of that Act, or the collection of information is one described in section 1-3(b) of that Act.\n\n**As amended, section 3507(a)(2) through (4) will read as follows** \u2014 new language marked as inserted, struck language marked. *This display is illustrative; the operative amendment is stated above.*\n\n> **(2)** the Director has approved the proposed collection of information or approval has been inferred, under the provisions of this section; ~~and~~\n>\n> **(3)** the agency has obtained from the Director a control number to be displayed upon the collection of information~~.~~**; and**\n>\n> **(4) in the case of a collection of information conducted in connection with a covered process, as that term is defined in section 0-3 of the Government Simplification Act, the agency is not, as to that covered process, an agency described in section 1-3(a) of that Act, or the collection of information is one described in section 1-3(b) of that Act.**"},{"anchor":"sec-8-4","heading":"\u00a7 8-4. Amendment to the Plain Writing Act of 2010.","part":"SECTION 8 \u2014 AMENDMENTS TO RELATED ACTS","body":"**The amendment.**\u2014Section 6 of the Plain Writing Act of 2010 (5 U.S.C. 301 note) is amended by adding at the end the following:\n\n**(c) Government Simplification Act.**\u2014Subsections (a) and (b) do not apply to a duty imposed by the Government Simplification Act, including the publication of the Government Burden Ledger under section 1-1 of that Act, the verification under section 1-2 of that Act of a certification under section 3506(c)(3)(D) of title 44, United States Code, a referral under section 4-1 of that Act recommending that a covered document be revised, and the response and docketing required by sections 4-2 through 4-4 of that Act. Nothing in this subsection creates a right or benefit enforceable by any person against an agency on the ground that a covered document is not written in plain writing, and a duty under that Act is enforceable only as that Act provides.\n\n**As amended, section 6 will read as follows** \u2014 new language marked as inserted. *This display is illustrative; the operative amendment is stated above.*\n\n> **SEC. 6. JUDICIAL REVIEW AND ENFORCEABILITY.**\n>\n> **(a) Judicial Review.**\u2014There shall be no judicial review of compliance or noncompliance with any provision of this Act.\n>\n> **(b) Enforceability.**\u2014No provision of this Act shall be construed to create any right or benefit, substantive or procedural, enforceable by any administrative or judicial action.\n>\n> **(c) Government Simplification Act.\u2014Subsections (a) and (b) do not apply to a duty imposed by the Government Simplification Act, including the publication of the Government Burden Ledger under section 1-1 of that Act, the verification under section 1-2 of that Act of a certification under section 3506(c)(3)(D) of title 44, United States Code, a referral under section 4-1 of that Act recommending that a covered document be revised, and the response and docketing required by sections 4-2 through 4-4 of that Act. Nothing in this subsection creates a right or benefit enforceable by any person against an agency on the ground that a covered document is not written in plain writing, and a duty under that Act is enforceable only as that Act provides.**"},{"anchor":"sec-8-5","heading":"\u00a7 8-5. Amendment to the 21st Century Integrated Digital Experience Act.","part":"SECTION 8 \u2014 AMENDMENTS TO RELATED ACTS","body":"**The amendment.**\u2014Section 4 of the 21st Century Integrated Digital Experience Act (44 U.S.C. 3501 note) is amended by adding at the end the following:\n\n**(f) Publication.**\u2014The head of each executive agency shall furnish the documentation required by subsection (d) to the Public Advocate for Government Simplification for each form in a covered process, as that term is defined in section 0-3 of the Government Simplification Act, and shall include it in the entry for that covered process under section 1-1(b)(10) of that Act. A form for which no documentation under subsection (d) has been furnished, and which is not available in a digital format meeting the requirements described in section 3(a), is reported in the Government Burden Ledger as a form not available in digital format for which no reason has been documented.\n\n**As amended, section 4 will conclude as follows** \u2014 new language marked as inserted. *This display is illustrative; the operative amendment is stated above.*\n\n> **(e) Physical Availability.**\u2014Each executive agency shall maintain an accessible method of completing digital services through in-person, paper-based, or other means, such that individuals without the ability to use digital services are not deprived of or impeded in access to those digital services.\n>\n> **(f) Publication.\u2014The head of each executive agency shall furnish the documentation required by subsection (d) to the Public Advocate for Government Simplification for each form in a covered process, as that term is defined in section 0-3 of the Government Simplification Act, and shall include it in the entry for that covered process under section 1-1(b)(10) of that Act. A form for which no documentation under subsection (d) has been furnished, and which is not available in a digital format meeting the requirements described in section 3(a), is reported in the Government Burden Ledger as a form not available in digital format for which no reason has been documented.**"},{"anchor":"sec-8-6","heading":"\u00a7 8-6. Conforming amendment to the Small Business Act \u2014 size from records.","part":"SECTION 8 \u2014 AMENDMENTS TO RELATED ACTS","body":"**The amendment.**\u2014Section 3(x) of the Small Business Act (15 U.S.C. 632(x)) is amended by adding at the end the following:\n\n**(3) Figures stated; comparison with records.**\u2014A certification under paragraph (1), and a registration described in subsection (w)(2)(C), shall state each figure by which the applicable size standard measures the concern, stating separately the concern's own figure and the figure including every concern with which it is affiliated. The concern's own figure is compared with records as section 4-9 of the Government Simplification Act provides, and a figure established under subsection (e) of that section is not required to be established again for the period to which the certification relates.\n\n**As amended, section 3(x) will read as follows** \u2014 new language marked as inserted. *This display is illustrative; the operative amendment is stated above.*\n\n> **(x) Annual certification**\n>\n> **(1) In general**\u2014Each business certified as a small business concern under this chapter shall annually certify its small business size and, if appropriate, its small business status, by means of a confirming entry on the Online Representations and Certifications Application database of the Administration, or any successor thereto.\n>\n> **(2) Regulations**\u2014Not later than 1 year after September 27, 2010, the Administrator, in consultation with the Inspector General and the Chief Counsel for Advocacy of the Administration, shall promulgate regulations to ensure that\u2014 (A) no business concern continues to be certified as a small business concern on the Online Representations and Certifications Application database of the Administration, or any successor thereto, without fulfilling the requirements for annual certification under this subsection; and (B) the requirements of this subsection are implemented in a manner presenting the least possible regulatory burden on small business concerns.\n>\n> **(3) Figures stated; comparison with records.\u2014A certification under paragraph (1), and a registration described in subsection (w)(2)(C), shall state each figure by which the applicable size standard measures the concern, stating separately the concern's own figure and the figure including every concern with which it is affiliated. The concern's own figure is compared with records as section 4-9 of the Government Simplification Act provides, and a figure established under subsection (e) of that section is not required to be established again for the period to which the certification relates.**"}],"asOf":"2026-11-12"}}}};
